(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3.78d856e543.js"]={"31":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which bacterium is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries?","choices":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica","D":"Campylobacter jejuni","E":"Shigella dysenteriae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-resource countries and an important cause of traveler's diarrhea and food poisoning, most often from improperly cooked chicken. Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the leading bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries. Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexico, and the Philippines and mainly affects travelers, not high-income populations at large. Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in northern and central Europe but is far less common than Campylobacter. Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries, whereas Campylobacter is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Campylobacter are comma-shaped, flagellated, gram-negative organisms diagnosed mainly by stool culture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection can be followed by reactive arthritis, mainly in HLA-B27–positive patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the lead","B":"Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in nor","E":"Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries, whe"}},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the known reservoir of Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Poultry","B":"Humans","C":"Shellfish","D":"Cattle","E":"Swine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Humans are the known reservoir of Shigella. Up to 10^9 organisms per gram of stool and an infective dose of several hundred organisms make fecal-oral spread by water and food very efficient. Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salmonella, whereas humans are the only known Shigella reservoir. Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae, not Shigella. Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella has no animal reservoir. Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shigella infects only humans. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella are gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonmotile facultative anaerobes closely related to enteroinvasive E. coli (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the United States, children in daycare, migrant workers, travelers, and nursing home residents are most affected (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salm","C":"Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae,","D":"Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella h","E":"Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shi"}},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal sites does Yersinia infection preferentially involve?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, sigmoid, and left colon","B":"Stomach, duodenum, and jejunum","C":"Ileum, appendix, and right colon","D":"Esophagus, stomach, and duodenum","E":"Jejunum, transverse colon, and rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Yersinia infections preferentially involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Organisms proliferate in lymphoid tissue, causing Peyer patch and regional node hyperplasia. The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of Shigella, not Yersinia, which targets the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel, localizing instead to the ileum, appendix, and right colon. The esophagus and stomach are not sites of Yersinia, which proliferates in lymphoid tissue of the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Yersinia does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or rectum; it localizes to the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and contaminated water and tend to cluster in winter (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of","B":"Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel, locali","D":"The esophagus and stomach are not sites of Yersinia, which p","E":"Yersinia does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or re"}},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) are the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea and spread via contaminated food or water. In under-resourced countries, children younger than 2 years are particularly susceptible. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef and HUS, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in young children in under-resourced countries, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and nursery outbreaks in children under 2, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea via biofilm, but ETEC remains the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ETEC are noninvasive and produce heat-labile and heat-stable toxins (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: like cholera, ETEC infection produces mild histologic changes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak blood","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in you","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and ","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhe"}},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and invades epithelial cells without producing toxins?","choices":{"A":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC) resemble Shigella bacteriologically. They do not produce toxins but invade epithelial cells and cause a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is not the toxin-free, Shigella-like invader. Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli without invading epithelial cells. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rather than invading epithelial cells. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC infections are most common among young children in under-resourced countries (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EIEC are transmitted via food, water, or person-to-person contact (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is n","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli wit","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rathe"}},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What does the heat-stable toxin of ETEC bind on enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase","B":"GM1 ganglioside receptor","C":"Rho family GTPases","D":"Guanylate cyclase","E":"Beta-1 integrin receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ETEC heat-stable toxin (ST) binds guanylate cyclase and raises intracellular cGMP; its effects on epithelial ion transport resemble those of the heat-labile toxin. Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action of the heat-labile toxin, not the heat-stable toxin. GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like heat-labile toxin, whereas heat-stable toxin acts on guanylate cyclase. Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC heat-stable toxin, which raises cGMP via guanylate cyclase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin resembles cholera toxin and activates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and chloride secretion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC are noninvasive, so mucosal biopsies show mild change (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action o","B":"GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like hea","C":"Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC hea"}},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What type of organism is Tropheryma whipplei?","choices":{"A":"Gram-negative curved spirochete","B":"Gram-positive actinomycete","C":"Acid-fast mycobacterial rod","D":"Flagellated intestinal protozoan","E":"Gram-negative comma-shaped rod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Whipple's original postmortem found foamy macrophages filled with gram-positive microbes; the organism was identified as an actinomycete in 1992 and named Tropheryma whipplei. T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actinomycete. T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated protozoan like Giardia. A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylobacter, not the gram-positive actinomycete T. whipplei. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tropheryma whipplei is an intracellular gram-positive organism (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: intact rod-shaped bacilli can be seen within macrophages by electron microscopy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actin","D":"T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated","E":"A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylo"}},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which stain result best separates Whipple disease from intestinal mycobacterial infection?","choices":{"A":"Positive mucicarmine stain","B":"Positive Gram stain result","C":"Negative acid-fast stain","D":"Positive Congo red stain","E":"Negative Giemsa stain result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both Whipple disease and intestinal mycobacterial infection show foamy macrophages with PAS-positive organisms. Mycobacteria are acid-fast positive, while T. whipplei is not. Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and does not separate Whipple disease from mycobacterial infection. Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it cannot distinguish Whipple disease from mycobacteria. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the intestinal lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: mycobacterial infection can mimic Whipple disease, particularly in an immunocompromised host (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and doe","D":"Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it "}},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which demographic group is Whipple disease most common in?","choices":{"A":"East Asian women","B":"West African children","C":"Native American teens","D":"South Asian older women","E":"Northern European men"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Whipple disease is most common in males of Northern European descent, particularly farmers and others with occupational exposure to soil or animals. Whipple disease is rare outside men of Northern European descent with soil or animal exposure, not East Asian women. Whipple disease affects mainly Northern European men, typically farmers, not West African children. Whipple disease is a disease of Northern European men with soil or animal exposure, not Native American teenagers. Whipple disease predominates in Northern European males, not South Asian older women. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease is most common in older males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Whipple disease is rare outside men of Northern European des","B":"Whipple disease affects mainly Northern European men, typica","C":"Whipple disease is a disease of Northern European men with s","D":"Whipple disease predominates in Northern European males, not"}},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which virus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide as rotavirus vaccine use has grown?","choices":{"A":"Norovirus","B":"Rotavirus","C":"Adenovirus","D":"Astrovirus","E":"Sapovirus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With increasing rotavirus vaccine use, norovirus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide, responsible for about 20% of acute gastroenteritis. Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is exactly why norovirus now leads as the cause of diarrhea worldwide. Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunistic infection but is not the leading cause worldwide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus is an icosahedral virus with a single-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infectious particles can be shed before symptoms and for up to 8 weeks afterward in healthy individuals (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is ex","C":"Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunist"}},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which age range is most vulnerable to rotavirus?","choices":{"A":"Birth to 6 months","B":"2 to 5 years","C":"5 to 12 years","D":"Over 65 years","E":"6 to 24 months"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because breast-milk antibodies protect during the first 6 months of life. Infants under 6 months are relatively protected by breast-milk antibodies, so peak vulnerability starts at 6 months. By 2 to 5 years children are past the peak window of 6 to 24 months. School-age children are well beyond the 6 to 24 month peak of rotavirus vulnerability. Older adults are not the main rotavirus risk group, which is children aged 6 to 24 months. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is an nonenveloped virus with a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the WHO recommends the first rotavirus vaccine dose at 6 weeks of age (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Infants under 6 months are relatively protected by breast-mi","B":"By 2 to 5 years children are past the peak window of 6 to 24","C":"School-age children are well beyond the 6 to 24 month peak o","D":"Older adults are not the main rotavirus risk group, which is"}},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the typical incubation period of adenovirus gastroenteritis?","choices":{"A":"About 12 hours","B":"About 2 days","C":"About 3 weeks","D":"About 1 week","E":"About 2 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenovirus disease typically presents after an incubation period of about 1 week, with diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal pain; symptoms generally resolve within 10 days. About 12 hours is far shorter than adenovirus incubation, which is about 1 week. About 2 days is closer to norovirus timing; adenovirus incubates for about 1 week. Three weeks is too long; adenovirus gastroenteritis appears after about 1 week and resolves within 10 days. Two months is far too long; adenovirus incubates for about 1 week and symptoms resolve within 10 days. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea that also affects older immunocompromised patients (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: fever and weight loss may accompany adenovirus gastroenteritis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"About 12 hours is far shorter than adenovirus incubation, wh","B":"About 2 days is closer to norovirus timing; adenovirus incub","C":"Three weeks is too long; adenovirus gastroenteritis appears ","E":"Two months is far too long; adenovirus incubates for about 1"}},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"How is giardiasis usually documented?","choices":{"A":"Modified acid-fast stool stain","B":"Colonic biopsy for trophozoites","C":"Stool cyst immunofluorescence","D":"Serum antiparasite IgG titers","E":"Stool culture on selective agar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Giardia infection is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples. Oral antimicrobial therapy is effective, but recurrence is common. A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, not Giardia. Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so colonic biopsy is not how giardiasis is documented. Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluorescence, not by bacterial culture on selective agar. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia trophozoites in duodenal biopsies are pear-shaped with two equally sized nuclei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infants, travelers, and immunocompromised patients have increased risk of severe giardiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, ","B":"Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so","E":"Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluores"}},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis?","choices":{"A":"Oral vancomycin","B":"Albendazole","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Nitazoxanide","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation, and is the most effective treatment for systemic disease. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis. Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment for the protozoan Entamoeba. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica. Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia, whereas metronidazole is the drug of choice for systemic amebiasis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. histolytica cysts have a chitin wall that resists gastric acid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: amebiasis most often affects the cecum and ascending colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis.","B":"Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment ","C":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan ","D":"Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia, wherea"}},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"What property lets Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated but unfiltered water?","choices":{"A":"Chlorine resistance","B":"Heat resistance","C":"Chitin acid shield","D":"Surface antigen variation","E":"Bile acid tolerance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are resistant to chlorine and persist in treated but unfiltered water; contaminated drinking water is the most common means of transmission. A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cryptosporidium persists in water because its oocysts resist chlorine. Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, not how Cryptosporidium survives water treatment. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosis of cryptosporidiosis is based on finding oocysts in the stool (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 1993 Milwaukee outbreak from inadequate water purification affected more than 400,000 people (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cry","D":"Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, no"}},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops ascending flaccid leg weakness and absent reflexes 2 weeks after diarrhea from undercooked chicken. What process most likely links the two illnesses?","choices":{"A":"Molecular mimicry of gangliosides","B":"Direct invasion of nerve roots","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release","D":"Immune complexes in muscle fibers","E":"HLA-B27 linked joint inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guillain-Barré syndrome after Campylobacter infection reflects molecular mimicry: antibodies to C. jejuni lipopolysaccharide cross-react with peripheral and central nervous system gangliosides. It is not HLA-linked. Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré follows antibody cross-reaction with gangliosides. Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which causes descending paralysis, not post-Campylobacter ascending weakness. Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascending weakness, which is a ganglioside-mimicry neuropathy. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 40% of Guillain-Barré cases have documented Campylobacter infection in the prior 2 weeks (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré fo","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which caus","D":"Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascendin"}},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman returns from the Philippines with fever, abdominal pain, and small salmon-colored macules on her chest. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods. What will her terminal ileum most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures","D":"Raised Peyer patches with oval ulcers","E":"Flattened villi with lymphocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Typhoid fever (S. typhi) invades small intestinal M cells. Peyer patches in the terminal ileum enlarge into plateau-like elevations, and mucosal damage creates oval ulcers oriented along the ileal axis that may perforate. Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not typhoid. Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile colitis, not typhoid ileitis. Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, not acute typhoid fever. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac disease, not Peyer patch hyperplasia from typhoid. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Typhoid fever). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rose spots on the chest and abdomen are a feature of typhoid fever (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: gallbladder colonization with S. typhi may be associated with gallstones and a chronic carrier state (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamo","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile c","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, n","E":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac"}},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in daycare has fever, cramps, and bloody diarrhea, and stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Oral rehydration","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics","C":"Antimotility agents","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Intravenous saline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antidiarrheal medications can prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance, so they are contraindicated. Antibiotic treatment shortens both the clinical course and the duration of bacterial shedding. Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysentery and should not be withheld. Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding of Shigella, so they are appropriate. Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrated child and is not contraindicated. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Shigellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella infections are most prominent in the left colon, and M-cell tropism can produce aphthous ulcers resembling Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Shigella dysenteriae serotype 1 releases Shiga toxin, which inhibits protein synthesis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — antidiarrheal drug classes and their adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysen","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding o","E":"Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrat"}},{"id":"sdl31_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea after a cookout with undercooked hamburgers. A week later she is pale, platelets are 40,000/µL (reference range 150,000–450,000/µL), and creatinine is rising. Which bacterial product most directly causes these complications?","choices":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin","B":"Heat-stable toxin","C":"Dispersin protein","D":"Shiga-like toxin","E":"Lipopolysaccharide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EHEC O157:H7 from undercooked ground beef produces Shiga-like toxins and is more likely than non-O157 serotypes to cause bloody diarrhea, hemolytic uremic syndrome, and ischemic colitis. Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS. Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC lesions show an ischemia-like morphology with surface atrophy and erosion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: antibiotic treatment can release Shiga-like toxins and raise hemolytic uremic syndrome risk, especially in children (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and ca","B":"Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and ca"}},{"id":"sdl31_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-month-old boy in a crowded urban daycare has 5 days of watery diarrhea. Research biopsy shows bacteria tightly attached to enterocyte apical membranes with local effacement of microvilli. Which group is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) produce attaching and effacing lesions: bacteria attach tightly to small intestinal enterocyte apical membranes and cause local effacement of microvilli. EPEC are a major cause of diarrhea in children under 2 years. Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attaching and effacing microvilli. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects the colon with bloody diarrhea, not small-intestinal watery diarrhea in a nursery. Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes dysentery rather than attaching and effacing. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather than effacing small-intestinal microvilli. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: assays for the bacterial proteins that insert into enterocyte membranes allow molecular diagnosis of EPEC (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EPEC are prevalent worldwide and cause endemic diarrhea and outbreaks (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attachin","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects t","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather "}},{"id":"sdl31_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man develops watery diarrhea 3 days after arriving in Mexico. Stool testing identifies an E. coli strain producing heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. What would a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","B":"Ischemic surface erosion","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers","D":"Foamy lamina macrophages","E":"Minimal mucosal changes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ETEC are noninvasive and act through toxins that raise cAMP and cGMP. Like cholera, ETEC infection results in mild histologic changes. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETEC is noninvasive and leaves little histologic change. Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, not toxin-mediated ETEC secretory diarrhea. Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not ETEC. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ETEC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin activates adenylate cyclase, while the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC spread through contaminated food or water (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETE","B":"Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, ","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, ","D":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ET"}},{"id":"sdl31_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man who farms in rural Wisconsin has diarrhea, weight loss, joint pain, and memory problems. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. What will special staining most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Acid-fast bacilli in macrophages","B":"PAS-positive lysosomal granules","C":"Congo red apple-green deposits","D":"Prussian blue iron granules","E":"Mucicarmine-staining capsules"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The macrophages of Whipple disease contain PAS-positive, diastase-resistant granules representing partially digested bacteria within lysosomes. T. whipplei is acid-fast negative, unlike mycobacteria. Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not the bacteria-laden macrophages of Whipple disease. Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T. whipplei within macrophages. Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Whipple macrophages are PAS-positive. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac symptoms, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms are common in Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the usual triad is diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not ","D":"Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T","E":"Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Wh"}},{"id":"sdl31_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man working on a dairy farm has steatorrhea, mesenteric lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria filled with foamy macrophages, and tissue PCR detects Tropheryma whipplei. What best explains his malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Impaired lymph drainage","B":"Brush-border enzyme loss","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","D":"Bile salt deconjugation","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages accumulate in the small intestinal lamina propria and mesenteric lymph nodes and obstruct lymphatics. The malabsorptive diarrhea results from impaired lymph drainage. Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villous damage, but Whipple malabsorption comes from lymphatic obstruction. Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreatic disease, not in Whipple disease. Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, not from macrophage-mediated lymphatic obstruction. Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera or ETEC, not Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and steatorrhea occur later in the course of Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the rarity of infection despite many healthy carriers suggests genetic risk factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villou","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreat","D":"Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, n","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera "}},{"id":"sdl31_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has had migratory joint pain for 2 years and now has diarrhea, weight loss, and a new heart murmur. Biopsy shows foamy macrophages expanding duodenal villi. What will endoscopy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions","C":"Yellow-white mucosal plaques","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dense macrophage infiltrates expand villi and give a shaggy surface. Lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition produce endoscopically visible white to yellow plaques. Macrophages can also accumulate in cardiac valves, synovium, and brain. Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not Whipple disease. Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whipple disease. Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, not Whipple disease. Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whipple disease shows yellow-white plaques. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the shaggy mucosal surface reflects villous expansion by dense macrophage infiltrates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms such as arthritis may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not ","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whippl","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, no","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whi"}},{"id":"sdl31_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman on a cruise ship develops vomiting and watery diarrhea during a shipwide outbreak and recovers in 2 days. Her 70-year-old husband, equally exposed, never becomes ill, and his saliva lacks blood group antigens. Which trait most likely protects him?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 positive genotype","B":"Heterozygous CFTR mutation","C":"Sickle hemoglobin trait carrier","D":"HLA-DQ2 haplotype expression","E":"Homozygous FUT2 inactivation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mutations inactivating FUT2 prevent fucosylation of blood group antigens that serve as norovirus binding sites. These homozygous nonsecretors are naturally resistant; about 20% of people of European and African descent carry this genotype. HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and other enteric infections, not norovirus resistance. Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk, not norovirus resistance. HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not a norovirus resistance factor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Norovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus outbreaks spread in schools, hospitals, nursing homes, and cruise ships through droplets, surfaces, and fomites (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: an infective dose of fewer than 20 viral particles makes prevention difficult (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and","C":"Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk","D":"HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not "}},{"id":"sdl31_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old unvaccinated boy has vomiting and watery diarrhea during a daycare outbreak. The virus has a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which viral factor may mediate the enterocyte damage?","choices":{"A":"Hemagglutinin-neuraminidase","B":"Nonstructural protein 4","C":"Reverse transcriptase","D":"Spike glycoprotein","E":"Envelope fusion protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes; damage may be mediated by nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis. Immature cells then repopulate the villus surface. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is about 10 viral particles (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus vaccines are incompletely effective in under-resourced regions, possibly due to malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl31_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man on immunosuppression after a heart transplant has 6 days of diarrhea, vomiting, and fever. Stool PCR detects adenovirus. What would a small intestinal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Prominent intranuclear viral inclusions","B":"Foamy macrophages in lamina propria","C":"Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adenovirus biopsies can show epithelial degeneration but more often show nonspecific villous atrophy and compensatory crypt hyperplasia. Viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not adenovirus enteritis. Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not adenovirus. Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not adenovirus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Adenovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: adenovirus symptoms generally resolve within 10 days (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — villi and microvilli as absorptive surface area","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ad","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not ade","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not a"}},{"id":"sdl31_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Mexico had bloody diarrhea a month ago and now has fever and right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a 9-cm liver abscess, and colon biopsy showed organisms ingesting red cells. How did the parasite most likely reach the liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent through bile ducts","B":"Lymphatic spread to liver","C":"Direct transmural extension","D":"Portal venous embolization","E":"Hepatic artery seeding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica invades crypts and burrows into the lamina propria, forming flask-shaped ulcers. Parasites penetrate splanchnic vessels and embolize to the liver, producing abscesses in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery. Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic splanchnic vessels and embolize through the portal vein. Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system, not lymphatics. Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain a liver abscess, which arises from portal embolization. Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal vein, not the hepatic artery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic liver abscesses can exceed 10 cm and have a shaggy fibrinous lining with scant marginal inflammation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: metronidazole is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic spl","B":"Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system","C":"Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain ","E":"Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal "}},{"id":"sdl31_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who drank stream water while camping has had intermittent bloating and greasy diarrhea for 4 months. Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped organisms with two nuclei on the brush border. Which parasite feature best explains her months of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Invasion into the lamina propria","B":"Chitin wall resisting acid","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites","E":"Variant surface protein switching"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giardia evade immune clearance through continuous modification of their major surface antigen, variant surface protein, and can persist for months or years with intermittent symptoms. They do not invade. Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attached to the brush border. A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and does not explain prolonged Giardia infection. Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, not Giardia. Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, not Giardia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Giardia lamblia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia decrease brush-border enzyme expression and cause microvillous damage and epithelial apoptosis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Giardia cysts resist chlorine, so infection is endemic in unfiltered public water supplies (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attache","B":"A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and d","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, n","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeb"}},{"id":"sdl31_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with advanced HIV has weeks of profuse watery diarrhea. Ileal biopsy shows small blue spheres that appear to sit on the brush border, and stool shows oocysts. Where does the organism actually reside?","choices":{"A":"Free in lumen over villi","B":"Vacuole within enterocytes","C":"Inside lamina propria macrophages","D":"Within submucosal lymphatics","E":"Inside flask-shaped ulcers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cryptosporidium sporozoites attach to the brush border and induce enterocytes to engulf them into an endocytic vacuole, so the intracellular parasite appears to sit on the apical membrane. Sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and tight junction leak cause watery diarrhea. Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen; it lies within an endocytic vacuole of the enterocyte. Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple disease or MAC, not Cryptosporidium. Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cryptosporidium. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Cryptosporidium). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptosporidium causes sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and increased tight junction permeability, producing watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in immunodeficient hosts, organisms can involve the biliary and respiratory tracts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen; it lies within an ","C":"Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple","E":"Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cry"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"An 18-year-old man has fever and right lower quadrant pain after eating undercooked pork in Germany. At surgery the appendix is mildly inflamed and mesenteric nodes are enlarged. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Yersinia enterocolitica","B":"Campylobacter jejuni","C":"Shigella sonnei","D":"Vibrio cholerae","E":"Salmonella enteritidis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and water, are more common in Europe, and involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Yersinia, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron enhances Yersinia virulence, so patients with hemochromatosis are at greater risk of sepsis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: postinfectious Yersinia complications include reactive arthritis and erythema nodosum (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea 3 days after a visit to a dairy farm, and stool grows E. coli O157:H7. Why should antibiotics generally be withheld?","choices":{"A":"Prolongs the asymptomatic carrier state","B":"Raises hemolytic uremic syndrome risk","C":"Selects for pseudomembranous colitis","D":"Masks a developing appendicitis","E":"Triggers a reactive arthritis flare"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antibiotic treatment of EHEC can lead to release of Shiga-like toxins and increased risk of hemolytic uremic syndrome, especially in children. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. coli O157:H7 outbreaks often come from inadequately cooked ground beef, reflecting a reservoir in cows (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: contaminated milk and vegetables are also vehicles for EHEC (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgias, and duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. Besides the gut and lymph nodes, where else can these macrophages accumulate?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle","B":"Thyroid gland","C":"Cardiac valves","D":"Renal glomeruli","E":"Adrenal cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages can accumulate within mesenteric lymph nodes, synovial membranes, cardiac valves, brain, and other sites. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the original Whipple case described malabsorption, lymphadenopathy, and arthritis of undefined origin (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: white to yellow endoscopic plaques reflect lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-month-old girl develops vomiting and watery diarrhea from rotavirus, but her exclusively breastfed 3-month-old brother stays well. What most likely protects the infant?","choices":{"A":"Immature villus receptors","B":"Higher gastric acidity","C":"Lower infective inoculum","D":"Faster enterocyte turnover","E":"Antibodies in breast milk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because antibodies in breast milk confer protection during the first 6 months of life. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus outbreaks in hospitals and daycare centers are common (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus has a short incubation period followed by several days of vomiting and watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman from Colombia has bloody diarrhea, and colon biopsy shows amoebas that resemble macrophages. How does the first-line drug for systemic disease act on this parasite?","choices":{"A":"Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis","B":"Binds 50S ribosomal subunits","C":"Blocks DNA gyrase activity","D":"Inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase","E":"Disrupts ergosterol synthesis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on glucose fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic dysentery forms flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck and broad base (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: liver abscesses develop in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has fever and diarrhea after a potluck egg salad, and stool culture grows nontyphoidal Salmonella. She is otherwise healthy. Why are antibiotics generally not recommended?","choices":{"A":"They can prolong the carrier state","B":"They suppress mucosal IgA secretion","C":"They trigger Guillain-Barré syndrome","D":"They cause a rebound toxin release","E":"They worsen secretory chloride loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For nontyphoidal Salmonella, antibiotic therapy is not generally recommended because it can prolong the carrier state or cause relapse and does not shorten diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Salmonella). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absent gastric acid, as in atrophic gastritis or acid suppression, lowers the Salmonella inoculum needed (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Salmonella infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What do endoscopic and microscopic evaluations show in irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Normal mucosal findings","B":"Patchy crypt abscesses","C":"Thickened collagen band","D":"Diffuse villous blunting","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite real symptoms, endoscopic and microscopic evaluations are normal in IBS, so the diagnosis rests on clinical symptoms and functional testing. Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, which must be excluded because IBS biopsies are normal. A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, not IBS, whose mucosa is normal. Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic cause excluded before diagnosing IBS. Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IBS shows no mucosal abnormality. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS has no structural abnormalities and is most common in middle-aged females (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS is divided into diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, and mixed subtypes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, ","C":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colit","D":"Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IB"}},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pair of conditions is associated with irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sacroiliitis and uveitis","B":"Gallstones and kidney stones","C":"Erythema nodosum and arthritis","D":"Fibromyalgia and depression","E":"Pyoderma and episcleritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Elentra objective lists fibromyalgia and mood disorders as IBS associations, and First Aid notes IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and mood disorders such as anxiety and depression. The distractors are extraintestinal or metabolic complications of inflammatory bowel disease. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (FA) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and with anxiety or depression (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: candidate IBS genes include serotonin reuptake transporters and cannabinoid receptors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"FA"},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucocorticoid course","B":"Lifestyle and diet changes","C":"Long-term opioid therapy","D":"Empiric antibiotic course","E":"Elective subtotal colectomy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elentra objective and First Aid both give lifestyle modification and dietary changes as first-line IBS treatment. Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease, and colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease. Long-term opioids are not IBS therapy; first-line care is lifestyle and diet change, with symptom-directed drugs such as loperamide only for IBS-D. Empiric antibiotics are not first-line for IBS, which starts with diet, fiber, exercise, and stress reduction. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-line IBS treatment is lifestyle modification and dietary change (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: 5-HT3 receptor antagonists are effective in many cases of diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Long-term opioids are not IBS therapy; first-line care is li","D":"Empiric antibiotics are not first-line for IBS, which starts"}},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene encoding an intracellular sensor of bacterial muramyl dipeptide is most strongly associated with Crohn disease?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"CFTR","C":"NOD2","D":"HFE","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NOD2 encodes an intracellular sensor of muramyl dipeptide and is the gene most strongly associated with Crohn disease in European populations, though risk alleles occur in about 30% of patients. NOD2 variants are protective in ulcerative colitis. APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and sporadic colon cancer, not Crohn disease. CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, which is linked to the bacterial sensor NOD2. HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn disease. KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a sensor of muramyl dipeptide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATG16L1 and IRGM, which act in autophagosome formation, are additional Crohn disease risk genes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: very early onset IBD can be monogenic, most often from IL10 or IL-10 receptor mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and spora","B":"CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, whi","D":"HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn di","E":"KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a"}},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which form of microscopic colitis shows a strong association with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Collagenous colitis","B":"Diversion colitis","C":"Ischemic colitis","D":"Ulcerative proctitis","E":"Lymphocytic colitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphocytic colitis is strongly associated with celiac disease and autoimmune diseases including Graves disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and autoimmune gastritis. Its collagen layer is of normal thickness, and intraepithelial lymphocytes often exceed 20 per 100 colonocytes. Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial collagen band, whereas the celiac-associated form is lymphocytic colitis with normal collagen. Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscopy, not a form of microscopic colitis. Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis with visible mucosal disease, not microscopic colitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis is most common in older females and has a normal endoscopic appearance (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: both forms of microscopic colitis cause chronic, nonbloody, watery diarrhea without weight loss (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial coll","C":"Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscop","D":"Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis"}},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibodies are often present in Crohn disease but typically absent in ulcerative colitis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Saccharomyces antibodies","B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease patients often have anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies, which are typically absent in ulcerative colitis. In contrast, up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients have p-ANCA. Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for celiac disease, not Crohn disease. Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease may be positive for anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies (ASCA) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: fecal calprotectin helps distinguish IBD activity from noninflammatory disease such as IBS (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangiti","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for c","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not"}},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colonic wall do sigmoid diverticula form?","choices":{"A":"Bases of colonic crypts","B":"Lymphoid aggregate sites","C":"Vasa recta entry points","D":"Mesenteric fat attachments","E":"Haustral fold crests"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Focal gaps in the inner circular muscle occur where nerves and arterial vasa recta penetrate it. In the colon these gaps are unreinforced, so raised intraluminal pressure herniates mucosa through them. Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through muscle gaps where vasa recta penetrate. Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula form at vasa recta entry gaps in the circular muscle. Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula herniate where vasa recta penetrate the inner circular muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur especially where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: colonic diverticula are 0.5 to 1 cm flask-like outpouchings that are uniformly spaced (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through mu","B":"Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula for","E":"Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula he"}},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of Western adults older than 60 years have colonic diverticular disease?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 50 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic diverticula are rare before age 30, but prevalence approaches 50% in Western adults older than 60 years. They are less common in Japan and under-resourced countries, probably from dietary differences. About 5 percent is far too low; prevalence approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. About 15 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. About 25 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. About 90 percent overestimates diverticular disease, which approaches 50% in Western adults over 60. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with obesity and diets low in fiber and high in total fat or red meat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: diverticula tend to occur in the right colon in Asian and African populations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent is far too low; prevalence approaches 50% in","B":"About 15 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which ","C":"About 25 percent underestimates diverticular disease, which ","D":"About 90 percent overestimates diverticular disease, which a"}},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the sex ratio of Meckel diverticula?","choices":{"A":"Twice as common in males","B":"Twice as common in females","C":"Equal in males and females","D":"Four times more in females","E":"Ten times more in males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By the rule of 2s, Meckel diverticula have a male-to-female ratio of 2:1, occur in about 2% of people, lie within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve, are about 2 inches long, and are most often symptomatic by age 2. Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females, per the rule of 2s. Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s gives a 2:1 male predominance. Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female excess is wrong. A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female ratio of Meckel diverticula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Meckel diverticulum extends from the antimesenteric side of the ileum and may contain ectopic pancreatic tissue (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: about 4% of Meckel diverticula ever become symptomatic (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females","C":"Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s give","D":"Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female exc","E":"A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female rati"}},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colon are hyperplastic polyps most commonly found?","choices":{"A":"Right colon","B":"Transverse colon","C":"Cecum and appendix","D":"Left colon","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are most common in the left colon, are typically less than 5 mm, and are frequently multiple in the sigmoid colon and rectum. The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps, whereas hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon. The transverse colon is not the typical site; hyperplastic polyps cluster in the sigmoid colon and rectum. Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectum rather than the cecum and appendix. The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps are colonic lesions mostly of the left colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are predominantly located in the rectosigmoid region (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colonic hyperplastic polyps are typically discovered in the sixth and seventh decades (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps","B":"The transverse colon is not the typical site; hyperplastic p","C":"Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectu","E":"The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps"}},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the proposed pathogenesis of colonic hyperplastic polyps?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic APC gene inactivation","B":"Germline STK11 gene mutation","C":"Repeated mucosal prolapse injury","D":"Mismatch repair gene silencing","E":"Delayed epithelial shedding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are thought to result from decreased epithelial turnover and delayed shedding of surface epithelial cells, leading to a piling up of goblet and absorptive cells. Mucosal prolapse drives solitary rectal ulcer polyps instead. Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic polyps are nonneoplastic and come from delayed epithelial shedding. Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not hyperplastic polyps. Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neoplasia, not the nonneoplastic hyperplastic polyp. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorptive cells with a serrated surface architecture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: epithelial hyperplasia can occur as a nonspecific reaction overlying any mass and may signal an adjacent lesion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic","B":"Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not","D":"Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neo"}},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What underlying abnormality causes solitary rectal ulcer syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prior pelvic radiation for carcinoma","B":"Poor anorectal sphincter relaxation","C":"Hypoperfusion of the rectal watershed","D":"Recurrent enteric bacterial infection","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impaired relaxation of the anorectal sphincter creates a sharp angle at the anterior rectal shelf, causing recurrent abrasion and ulceration of the overlying mucosa. Entrapment of the resulting polyp in the fecal stream leads to mucosal prolapse. Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, which comes from poor sphincter relaxation. Recurrent infection is not the cause; solitary rectal ulcer results from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and mucosal prolapse. Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Meckel diverticulum, not of solitary rectal ulcer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer syndrome presents with rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and an inflammatory lesion of the anterior rectal wall (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: inflammatory polyps form through repeated cycles of injury and healing (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not so","D":"Recurrent infection is not the cause; solitary rectal ulcer ","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Mecke"}},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene is the most common mutation identified in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"KRAS","C":"MUTYH","D":"SMAD4","E":"PTEN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SMAD4, which encodes a signaling intermediate in the TGF-beta pathway, is the most common mutation in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. BMPR1A mutations account for other cases, and together they explain fewer than half of patients. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile polyposis. KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline cause of juvenile polyposis. MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous polyposis, not autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juvenile polyposis most often involves SMAD4. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis is an autosomal dominant syndrome of numerous hamartomatous polyps in the colon, stomach, and small bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: patients with SMAD4 mutations often have both juvenile polyposis and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile p","B":"KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline c","C":"MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous pol","E":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juv"}},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Where are the polyps of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Small intestine","C":"Rectosigmoid colon","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are most common in the small intestine, although they may occur in the stomach and colon and rarely in the bladder and lungs. Grossly they are large, pedunculated, and lobulated. Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most common in the small intestine. Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in the small intestine. The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers polyps, which favor the small intestine. The ascending colon is a less common site; Peutz-Jeghers polyps favor the small intestine over the colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome features numerous hamartomatous polyps throughout the GI tract with hyperpigmented macules (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome presents at a median age of 11 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most c","C":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in th","D":"The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers ","E":"The ascending colon is a less common site; Peutz-Jeghers pol"}},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway does the Peutz-Jeghers tumor suppressor STK11 regulate?","choices":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"AMPK signaling","D":"DNA mismatch repair","E":"Hedgehog signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"STK11 is a tumor suppressor that regulates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) pathways, which control cell polarization and brake growth and anabolic metabolism. Germline heterozygous loss-of-function STK11 mutations occur in about half of familial cases. BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in juvenile polyposis (BMPR1A), not the STK11 pathway. Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers. DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-Jeghers, whose STK11 regulates AMPK. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: the second normal STK11 copy is often lost through somatic mutation in Peutz-Jeghers-associated cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in ju","B":"Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by S","D":"DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-"}},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has crampy lower abdominal pain on about 5 days each month for 6 months, with bloating and alternating loose and hard stools. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which additional feature best supports the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools","B":"Weight loss over 10%","C":"Elevated stool calprotectin","D":"Pain eased by defecation","E":"Symptom onset after age 60"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins lists IBS criteria as abdominal pain or discomfort at least 3 days per month over 3 months with improvement after defecation and a change in stool frequency or form, after other causes are excluded. An elevated fecal calprotectin points toward organic disease. Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic disease such as IBD, not support for IBS. Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be excluded, not IBS. Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, whereas IBS typically begins in younger adults. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome, Inflammatory bowel diseases). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is recurrent abdominal pain with at least 2 of: related to defecation, change in stool frequency, change in stool form, so under FA no single feature is mandatory (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS prevalence in high-income countries is about 5% to 10%, peaking between 20 and 40 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility, haustral contractions, and mass movements","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic dise","B":"Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be e","E":"Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, where"}},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has 2 years of urgent loose stools and intermittent abdominal cramping. Colonoscopy is normal, and he does not have celiac disease. A test shows excess fecal bile acids. Which mechanism best explains this subtype of his disorder?","choices":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury","B":"Subepithelial collagen band","C":"Bile acid malabsorption","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess bile acid synthesis or bile acid malabsorption is one identified cause of diarrhea-predominant IBS, likely through effects of bile acids on intestinal motility and epithelial ion transport. Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has already been excluded. A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which a normal colonoscopy with biopsy would reveal, not IBS. Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory colitis with abnormal biopsies, not IBS. Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be visible on colonoscopy, unlike IBS. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea-predominant IBS shows increased colonic contractions and transit rates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: opioids and anticholinergic psychoactive drugs are also used for diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has a","B":"A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which ","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory coliti","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be"}},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman returns from Mexico after a week of fever and watery diarrhea. Six months later she has recurring cramps, bloating, and variable stool form with normal colonoscopy and biopsies. Which mechanism is proposed for this onset pattern?","choices":{"A":"Shift in the gut microbiome","B":"Antibodies to enteric neurons","C":"Persistent mucosal granulomas","D":"Chronic bacterial tissue invasion","E":"Subepithelial collagen deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some IBS patients relate onset to a bout of infectious gastroenteritis, suggesting that immune activation or a shift in the gut microbiome triggers disease. Granulomas, invasion, and collagen deposition would produce abnormal biopsies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is chronic and may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: defective brain-gut axis signaling is proposed in IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 8 months of right lower quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a draining perianal tract. Resection of a strictured terminal ileum shows a thick rubbery wall with deep fissures. Which additional gross finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Continuous disease of mucosa","B":"Thin wall with smooth serosa","C":"Superficial broad ulcers","D":"Flat atrophic mucosal surface","E":"Creeping fat over the serosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In transmural Crohn disease, mesenteric adipose tissue frequently extends over the serosal surface as creeping fat. Wall thickening from edema, fibrosis, and muscular hypertrophy drives strictures. Continuous mucosal disease with a normal serosa fits ulcerative colitis. A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, whose transmural inflammation thickens the wall and draws creeping fat. Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, whereas Crohn disease has deep linear fissuring ulcers. A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which shows cobblestoning from deep ulcers between spared mucosa. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease shows cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, and a string sign on small bowel follow-through (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: disease often recurs at the anastomosis, and up to 40% need more resection within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, wh","C":"Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative col","D":"A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which sh"}},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with newly diagnosed ulcerative colitis has pruritus and an alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (reference range 44–147 U/L). MRCP shows beaded intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. When should colonoscopic dysplasia surveillance begin?","choices":{"A":"About 8 years after diagnosis","B":"At the time of diagnosis","C":"Beginning at 50 years of age","D":"After the first bloody relapse","E":"Upon confirmation of pancolitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with IBD typically enter surveillance about 8 years after diagnosis. The major exception is IBD with primary sclerosing cholangitis, which carries greater cancer risk, so surveillance starts at diagnosis. Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associated IBD requires surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warrants surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis regardless of disease extent. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease) (Colitis-associated neoplasia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: colitis-associated cancer risk rises sharply 8 to 10 years after onset (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholestatic disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associat","D":"Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warra","E":"Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis r"}},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has 4 months of watery, nonbloody diarrhea without weight loss. Colonoscopy is normal, but biopsies show more than 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes with a collagen layer of normal thickness. Her medications are listed. Which is most likely contributing?","choices":{"A":"Metformin","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Levothyroxine","D":"Naproxen","E":"Amlodipine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These biopsies show lymphocytic microscopic colitis. Similar histologic changes can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis histology shows lamina propria lymphocytes with intraepithelial lymphocytosis or a thickened subepithelial collagen band (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: collagenous colitis has a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — large-intestine lamina propria and the collagen layer","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man had left lower quadrant pain and fever with a WBC of 16,000/µL (reference range 4,500–11,000/µL) 2 months ago. He now has recurrent urinary tract infections and air in his urine. Which complication best explains his current findings?","choices":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Ischemic stricture","D":"Free perforation","E":"Colovesical fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inflammation in sigmoid diverticula can extend through the thin wall to cause serositis, abscesses, sinus tracts, and perforation. A tract into the bladder produces pneumaturia and recurrent infection. Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia and recurrent urinary infections. Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air in the urine. An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into the bladder producing pneumaturia. Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic pneumaturia of a colovesical fistula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis complications include abscess, colovesical fistula with pneumaturia, obstruction, and perforation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: surgery is generally reserved for severe or recurrent diverticulitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia a","B":"Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air ","C":"An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into t","D":"Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic p"}},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a lifelong low-fiber diet has dozens of sigmoid outpouchings on colonoscopy, but small bowel imaging shows no outpouchings. Which structural difference best explains the sparing of her small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Lower intraluminal small bowel pressure","B":"Absence of penetrating vasa recta","C":"Denser submucosal lymphoid tissue","D":"Continuous longitudinal muscle coat","E":"Thicker muscularis mucosae layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Both bowels have gaps in the inner circular muscle where nerves and vasa recta penetrate. In the small intestine these gaps are reinforced by a continuous outer longitudinal layer, whereas in the colon that layer is gathered into taeniae coli. Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bowel's continuous longitudinal coat reinforces the muscle gaps. Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their absence does not explain small bowel sparing. The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps; the continuous outer longitudinal layer does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is caused by intraluminal pressure and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: hypertrophy of the inner circular muscle is commonly associated with diverticulosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bo","B":"Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their ab","E":"The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps; the "}},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy has episodes of screaming with legs drawn up, vomiting, and a stool of blood and mucus. A sausage-shaped right abdominal mass is palpated. At surgery, a congenital ileal pouch lined by gastric mucosa is found at the leading point. Which complication of this pouch explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Diverticulitis","B":"Intussusception","C":"Small bowel volvulus","D":"Peptic perforation","E":"Adhesive obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Meckel diverticulum can act as a lead point for intussusception and can also cause hematochezia, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum. Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis but does not produce a sausage-shaped mass with currant-jelly stool. Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction but not a sausage-shaped mass with blood and mucus at a lead point. Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes peritonitis, not the telescoping mass of intussusception. Adhesive obstruction follows surgery and does not produce a sausage-shaped mass with currant-jelly stool. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause painless hematochezia, RLQ pain, intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: symptomatic Meckel cases can mimic acute appendicitis or obstruction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis but does not produ","C":"Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction but not a sausage-","D":"Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes perito","E":"Adhesive obstruction follows surgery and does not produce a "}},{"id":"sdl32_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps. Histology shows mature goblet and absorptive cells with a saw-tooth outline limited to the upper third of the crypts. Which premalignant lesion must be distinguished from these?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Juvenile hamartoma","C":"Sessile serrated polyp","D":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","E":"Lymphoid aggregate polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, but they must be distinguished from sessile serrated polyps, which are histologically similar and premalignant. A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not mimic the saw-tooth pattern of a hyperplastic polyp. A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not serrated crypt tops, and is not the key mimic. An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mucosa, not a premalignant serrated mimic. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps show a saw-tooth crypt pattern, BRAF mutations, and CpG island methylation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps may occur singly but are frequently multiple (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not ","B":"A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not ser","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mu"}},{"id":"sdl32_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with 15 years of ulcerative pancolitis has colonoscopy showing many mucosal projections, some joined at their tips into bridges. Biopsies show no dysplasia. How do these projections form?","choices":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth","B":"Clonal adenomatous epithelial growth","C":"Submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands","E":"Regrowth of mucosa between ulcers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In ulcerative colitis, isolated islands of regenerating mucosa bulge into the lumen to create pseudopolyps, and their tips may fuse into mucosal bridges. Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or juvenile polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these biopsies show none; pseudopolyps are regenerating mucosa. Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retention polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory pseudopolyps are due to mucosal erosion in inflammatory bowel disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: pseudopolyps are marked in ulcerative colitis and moderate in Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or ","B":"Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these bio","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retentio"}},{"id":"sdl32_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and chronic straining. A polypoid lesion is on the anterior rectal wall. Biopsy shows erosion and epithelial hyperplasia. Which additional histologic feature indicates mucosal prolapse?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria smooth muscle","B":"Noncaseating submucosal granulomas","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen","D":"Apoptotic bodies in crypt bases","E":"Foamy macrophages in lamina"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome polyps show mixed inflammation, erosion, and epithelial hyperplasia with prolapse-induced fibromuscular hyperplasia of the lamina propria. Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal prolapse. Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not prolapse. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mucosal prolapse. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer polyps are an example of an inflammatory lesion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: granulation tissue-like capillary proliferation in the lamina propria reflects repeated erosion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal p","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not","E":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mu"}},{"id":"sdl32_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has painless rectal bleeding, and a red mass prolapses through the anus. The resected pedunculated polyp is smooth, with cystic glands filled with mucin and inflammatory debris in an expanded lamina propria. He has no family history of polyps. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","B":"Juvenile retention polyp","C":"Tubulovillous adenoma","D":"Sessile hyperplastic polyp","E":"Submucosal leiomyoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps, also called retention polyps, are usually solitary, often rectal, and occur mostly before age 5. They show cystic mucin-filled glands in inflamed lamina propria and can prolapse through the anal sphincter. An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which this child lacks. A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, not a cystic mucin-filled polyp in a 4-year-old. A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not cystic mucin-filled glands. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary hamartomatous polyps do not have significant risk of transformation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: dysplasia is rare in sporadic juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which thi","C":"A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, no","D":"A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not "}},{"id":"sdl32_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has dozens of hamartomatous polyps in the colon, recurrent nosebleeds, lip telangiectasias, and a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. Her father had similar polyps. Which malignancy is she at greatest risk of developing by her mid-40s?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma","C":"Colonic adenocarcinoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic islet tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with dysplasia in polyps and separate adenomas, and 30% to 50% of patients develop colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations are recognized extraintestinal features. Hepatocellular carcinoma is not a juvenile polyposis risk; the major risk is colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pancreatic islet tumors are not juvenile polyposis features; colonic adenocarcinoma is the main risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with increased risk of colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colectomy may be needed to limit chronic hemorrhage from ulcerated juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is not a juvenile polyposis risk; t","E":"Pancreatic islet tumors are not juvenile polyposis features;"}},{"id":"sdl32_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has dark blue-brown macules on his lips, nostrils, and palms, and imaging shows multiple GI polyps. A resected polyp has an arborizing network of smooth muscle and normal epithelium. Which neoplasm warrants surveillance starting at birth?","choices":{"A":"Sex cord stromal tumor","B":"Gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Small intestinal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma","E":"Lung adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries a lifetime cancer risk near 40%. Surveillance begins at birth for testicular sex cord tumors, in late childhood for gastric and small intestinal cancers, and in the second and third decades for colon, pancreatic, breast, lung, ovarian, and uterine cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries increased risk of breast and GI cancers, including colorectal, gastric, small bowel, and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: smooth muscle mixed with lamina propria distinguishes Peutz-Jeghers polyps from juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has 1 year of bloating, hard infrequent stools, and abdominal pain. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which motility change is typical of her subtype?","choices":{"A":"Accelerated colonic transit","B":"Mucosal crypt destruction","C":"Slowed colonic transit","D":"Absent rectal ganglion cells","E":"Bile acid overproduction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patients with constipation-predominant IBS tend to have decreased colonic contractions and transit rates, while diarrhea-predominant patients show increased rates. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS pathophysiology is multifaceted, and chronic symptoms may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: psychologic stressors, diet, and increased enteric sensory responses contribute to IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man develops bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus. Colonoscopy shows continuous mucosal inflammation from the distal colon upward. Which recent habit change most likely preceded his onset?","choices":{"A":"Quitting cigarette smoking","B":"Starting oral contraceptives","C":"Starting daily NSAID use","D":"Adopting a high fiber diet","E":"Beginning heavy alcohol use"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike Crohn disease, onset of ulcerative colitis can be associated with smoking cessation, and smoking may give partial symptomatic relief. Smoking is a strong risk factor for Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ulcerative colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is treated with 5-aminosalicylic acid preparations, 6-mercaptopurine, infliximab, or colectomy (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: most ulcerative colitis patients have at least one relapse within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman learns at screening colonoscopy that she has many sigmoid outpouchings. She has no abdominal symptoms and asks how often people with this finding develop symptoms. What is the best estimate?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 20 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most individuals with diverticular disease remain asymptomatic, but about 20% develop intermittent cramping, lower abdominal discomfort, constipation, distention, or tenesmus. Even diverticulitis most often resolves spontaneously. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis classically causes LLQ pain, fever, and leukocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: diverticular disease can cause alternating constipation and diarrhea that mimics IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps on screening colonoscopy, and biopsies show mature goblet and absorptive cells. What size are these lesions typically?","choices":{"A":"About 1 to 2 cm","B":"About 2 to 3 cm","C":"About 3 to 5 cm","D":"More than 5 cm","E":"Under 5 mm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are typically less than 5 mm in diameter and appear as smooth, nodular protrusions, often on the crests of mucosal folds. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are generally small (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: crypt base epithelium of hyperplastic polyps may have a regenerative appearance (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl has colicky abdominal pain from multiple hamartomatous GI polyps and dark brown macules on her lips and fingers. Her mother says the spots look like freckles. Which feature distinguishes these macules from freckles?","choices":{"A":"Involvement of the oral mucosa","B":"Darkening with sun exposure","C":"Raised verrucous surface","D":"Blanching with pressure","E":"Symmetric facial distribution"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers macules are dark blue to brown and appear on the lips, nostrils, buccal mucosa, palms, genitalia, and perianal region. They resemble freckles but are distinguished by buccal mucosal involvement. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Peutz-Jeghers syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers hyperpigmented macules involve the mouth, lips, hands, and genitalia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: STK11 testing helps diagnose patients with polyps who lack mucocutaneous pigmentation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with Crohn disease has resection of a fibrotic terminal ileal stricture. The surgeon plans to remove as little bowel as possible. Which outcome makes this bowel preservation important?","choices":{"A":"Postoperative toxic megacolon","B":"Loss of colonic water absorption","C":"Adhesive small bowel obstruction","D":"Recurrence at the anastomosis","E":"Postsurgical bile reflux gastritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crohn disease often recurs at the site of anastomosis, and up to 40% of patients need additional resections within 10 years, so bowel preservation is an important part of surgical planning. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Crohn disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease is treated with glucocorticoids, azathioprine, antibiotics, and biologics such as infliximab and adalimumab (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: extensive small bowel Crohn disease can cause hypoalbuminemia and malabsorption of vitamin B12 and bile salts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"}],"33":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic hallmark defines colorectal adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Crypt base serration","B":"Mucosal prolapse fibrosis","C":"Epithelial dysplasia","D":"Granulomatous inflammation","E":"Hamartomatous smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hallmark of colorectal adenomas is epithelial dysplasia, seen as nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and stratification, often with prominent nucleoli and fewer goblet cells. Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile serrated polyps, whereas adenomas are defined by epithelial dysplasia. Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ulcer polyps, not adenomas. Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an adenoma. Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are neoplastic and arise via the chromosomal instability pathway with APC and KRAS mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colorectal adenomas range from 0.3 to 10 cm and have a velvet or raspberry-like surface (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile se","B":"Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ","D":"Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an ad","E":"Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, n"}},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of colorectal adenomas larger than 4 cm contain foci of invasive cancer?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 40 percent","C":"About 15 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy risk in an adenoma. Cancer is rare in adenomas under 1 cm, while nearly 40% of lesions larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. About 5 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. About 15 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm harbor invasive cancer. About 75 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. About 95 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas larger than 4 cm contain invasive cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous histology carries more malignant potential than tubular histology, and tubulovillous is intermediate (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular adenomas, partly because they tend to be larger (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of adeno","C":"About 15 percent underestimates the risk; nearly 40% of aden","D":"About 75 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas","E":"About 95 percent overstates the risk; nearly 40% of adenomas"}},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does intramucosal carcinoma within an adenoma carry little metastatic potential?","choices":{"A":"Absent mucosal lymphatics","B":"Intact muscularis propria","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Functional lymphatic channels are absent in the colonic mucosa, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential and complete polypectomy is generally curative. Invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae carries a risk of spread. The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal lymphatics limits spread, and invasion past the muscularis mucosae already carries risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomas are usually asymptomatic but may present with occult bleeding (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: invasion into the submucosal stalk of a pedunculated polyp constitutes invasive adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal l"}},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"How many adenomas are needed for a diagnosis of classic familial adenomatous polyposis?","choices":{"A":"At least 10","B":"At least 25","C":"At least 100","D":"At least 50","E":"At least 1000"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At least 100 polyps are necessary for a diagnosis of classic FAP, though thousands may be present. Apart from their number, FAP polyps look like sporadic adenomas. Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100. Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of at least 100. Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for classic FAP. Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at least 100, not 1000. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP polyps arise after puberty, are pancolonic, and involve the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: about 75% of FAP cases are inherited and the remainder arise from de novo mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100.","B":"Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of a","D":"Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for","E":"Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at"}},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the protein product of the gene mutated in familial adenomatous polyposis normally do?","choices":{"A":"Repairs base mismatches","B":"Activates RAS signaling","C":"Inhibits mTOR signaling","D":"Blocks TGF beta signals","E":"Degrades beta catenin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"APC is a key negative regulator of Wnt signaling. It binds and promotes degradation of beta catenin, so its loss lets beta catenin enter the nucleus and drive MYC and cyclin D1 transcription. Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair genes such as MSH2 and MLH1, not APC. APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while KRAS activation is a separate later step in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers, not APC. APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as SMAD4 are lost later in the sequence or in juvenile polyposis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP is an autosomal dominant mutation of the APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancers without APC mutations often carry beta catenin mutations that resist APC-dependent degradation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair gene","B":"APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while K","C":"Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghe","D":"APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as "}},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extraintestinal manifestation of familial adenomatous polyposis can be detected at birth?","choices":{"A":"Multiple mandibular osteomas","B":"Abdominal wall desmoid tumors","C":"Multiple epidermoid skin cysts","D":"Retinal pigment hypertrophy","E":"Impacted supernumerary teeth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium can be detected at birth and may be an adjunct to early FAP screening. Osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental anomalies appear later in variants such as Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gardner syndrome adds osseous and soft tissue tumors and impacted or supernumerary teeth to FAP (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: specific APC mutations partly explain FAP variants (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which genes are mutated in most patients with Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"APC or CTNNB1","B":"BRCA1 or BRCA2","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A","D":"KRAS or BRAF","E":"MSH2 or MLH1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"There are at least five mismatch repair genes, but most HNPCC patients have mutations in MSH2 or MLH1. Patients inherit one mutant and one normal allele. APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves mismatch repair genes. SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lynch syndrome. KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tumors, not the germline defect in Lynch syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is an autosomal dominant mismatch repair defect with about 80% progression to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: the human genome has about 50,000 to 100,000 microsatellites (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves ","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lync","D":"KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tum"}},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of all colorectal cancers are attributed to hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer?","choices":{"A":"About 10 to 15 percent","B":"About 2 to 4 percent","C":"About 20 to 25 percent","D":"About 40 to 50 percent","E":"About 70 to 80 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HNPCC accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers, making it the most common syndromic form of colon cancer. Its cancers occur at younger ages than sporadic colon cancers. Ten to 15 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers. Twenty to 25 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers. Forty to 50 percent grossly overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about 2% to 4% of colorectal cancers. Seventy to 80 percent is roughly the share of sporadic APC-pathway cancers, not HNPCC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is associated with endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: HNPCC patients have fewer polyps and develop cancer later than FAP patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Ten to 15 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for about","C":"Twenty to 25 percent overstates HNPCC, which accounts for ab","D":"Forty to 50 percent grossly overstates HNPCC, which accounts","E":"Seventy to 80 percent is roughly the share of sporadic APC-p"}},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which colonic region is characteristically involved in Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Rectal ampulla","C":"Proximal colon","D":"Splenic flexure","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The objective and First Aid place Lynch syndrome in the proximal colon, and Robbins notes HNPCC colon cancers are often in the right colon. The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site, whereas Lynch cancers favor the proximal colon. The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor the cecum and ascending colon. The splenic flexure is not characteristic; Lynch syndrome involves the proximal colon. The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome favors the right colon. Sources: SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3; Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal colon is involved in Lynch syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: microsatellite unstable and CIMP tumors are frequently located in the right colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site, whereas Lynch cancer","B":"The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor t","D":"The splenic flexure is not characteristic; Lynch syndrome in","E":"The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome f"}},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"At what age does colorectal cancer incidence peak?","choices":{"A":"20 to 30 years","B":"30 to 40 years","C":"40 to 50 years","D":"60 to 70 years","E":"80 to 90 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colorectal cancer incidence peaks at 60 to 70 years of age. Fewer than 20% of cases occur before age 50, though incidence before age 40 is rising. Colorectal cancer is uncommon at 20 to 30 years; incidence peaks at 60 to 70. Thirty to 40 years is too young; incidence peaks at 60 to 70 years. Forty to 50 years is too early, as fewer than 20% of cases occur before age 50. Eighty to 90 years is past the peak, which is 60 to 70 years. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most colorectal cancer patients are older than 50 years, and about 25% have a family history (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: colorectal adenocarcinoma causes nearly 15% of US cancer deaths, second to lung cancer (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colorectal cancer is uncommon at 20 to 30 years; incidence p","B":"Thirty to 40 years is too young; incidence peaks at 60 to 70","C":"Forty to 50 years is too early, as fewer than 20% of cases o","E":"Eighty to 90 years is past the peak, which is 60 to 70 years"}},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern is most closely associated with increased colorectal cancer risk?","choices":{"A":"Low vegetable fiber diet","B":"High citrus fruit intake","C":"Low red meat consumption","D":"High fish oil consumption","E":"Low refined sugar intake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low intake of unabsorbable vegetable fiber with high intake of refined carbohydrates and fat is most closely tied to colorectal cancer. Reduced stool bulk may prolong mucosal contact with bacterial oxidative byproducts. Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake raises colorectal cancer risk. Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohydrate intake is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include adenomatous and serrated polyps, IBD, tobacco use, and processed meat with low fiber (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high fat intake raises hepatic bile acid synthesis, and bacteria can convert bile acids into carcinogens (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake ra","E":"Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohy"}},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which organ is the most common site of distant metastasis from colon cancer?","choices":{"A":"Lung","B":"Liver","C":"Bone","D":"Brain","E":"Ovary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the colon drains through the portal circulation, the liver is the most common site of distant metastases. Regional nodes, lungs, and bones can also be involved. Lungs can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver the most common distant site. Bone can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver the most common distant site. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectosigmoid is the most common colorectal cancer site, followed by ascending and descending colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the rectum does not drain via the portal circulation, so metastatic anal carcinomas often circumvent the portal route (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Lungs can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver t","C":"Bone can be involved, but portal drainage makes the liver th"}},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Deep tenderness two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine is known by what name?","choices":{"A":"Rovsing sign","B":"Psoas sign","C":"Murphy sign","D":"McBurney sign","E":"Obturator sign"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The McBurney sign is deep tenderness two-thirds of the distance from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine, a classic finding in acute appendicitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute appendicitis may also elicit psoas, obturator, and Rovsing signs (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: acute appendicitis has a lifetime risk of 7% and affects males slightly more often (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common tumor of the appendix?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous cystadenoma","B":"Signet ring carcinoma","C":"Burkitt lymphoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common appendiceal tumor. It is usually incidental, forms a bulbous swelling at the distal tip, and rarely spreads. Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common than the well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs such as octreotide are used to treat carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Octreotide). COMLEX: conventional adenomas and adenocarcinomas of the appendix can cause obstruction that mimics acute appendicitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common"}},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major risk factor for HPV-associated squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal?","choices":{"A":"Receptive anal intercourse","B":"Chronic laxative overuse","C":"Portal venous hypertension","D":"Low dietary fiber intake","E":"Untreated ulcerative colitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anal squamous cell carcinoma is frequently associated with high-risk HPV strains, and the major risk factor for these HPV-associated lesions is anal sex. Low-risk HPV strains cause condyloma acuminatum. Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-related anal squamous carcinoma. Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a risk for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma. Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not HPV-associated anal squamous carcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Anal canal). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma arises below the pectinate line, while adenocarcinoma arises above it (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: basaloid anal squamous cell carcinoma has been called cloacogenic carcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-rela","D":"Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a ","E":"Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not H"}},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile colonic polyp with long, slender surface projections on biopsy. Her daughter asks what best predicts whether such a growth harbors cancer. Which feature is most important?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell number","B":"Degree of inflammation","C":"Lesion diameter","D":"Tubular gland pattern","E":"Patient sex and age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy in an adenoma. Villous architecture correlates with cancer partly because villous adenomas are larger, and architectural category has little significance in isolation. Architectural pattern has little significance alone; size is the main predictor of cancer in an adenoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: tubular adenomas tend to be small pedunculated polyps of rounded glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Architectural pattern has little significance alone; size is"}},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has three colonic adenomas removed. One shows high-grade dysplasia and the others show low-grade dysplasia. How does the high-grade lesion affect cancer risk in his remaining polyps?","choices":{"A":"Higher risk in other polyps","B":"Unchanged risk in other polyps","C":"Field cancerization of colon","D":"Germline repair defect implied","E":"Doubled risk in other polyps"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-grade dysplasia is a risk factor for cancer in that individual polyp but does not increase cancer risk in other polyps within that patient. High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in his other polyps. High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; it affects only the polyp containing it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps progress through an adenoma-carcinoma sequence with APC loss followed by KRAS mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high-grade dysplasia raises cancer risk within the individual polyp (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — chronic reflux injury and Barrett dysplasia","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in ","E":"High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; i"}},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has small adenomas removed at each of several surveillance endoscopies. She asks why repeated procedures lower her colon cancer risk. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"Ablation of mucosal lymphatics","B":"Reversal of APC mutations","C":"Induction of mucosal immunity","D":"Removal of precursor polyps","E":"Shrinkage of hyperplastic polyps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenomas are precursors of colorectal adenocarcinoma, and removed polyps do not progress to adenocarcinoma, so repeated surveillance is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Most adenomas do not progress, and no tool identifies those that will. Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by removing adenomas before they progress. Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations; it removes precursor polyps. Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinking them would not lower cancer risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: average-risk screening begins at age 45 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: adenomas develop in about 30% of Western adults by age 60 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by re","B":"Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations; it removes precursor","E":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinkin"}},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with familial adenomatous polyposis had a prophylactic colectomy at age 20. Which site most needs continued endoscopic surveillance for adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Distal esophagus","C":"Gallbladder fundus","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Hepatic hilum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colectomy prevents colorectal cancer in FAP, but patients remain at risk elsewhere. The ampulla of Vater and the stomach are common extracolonic sites of adenomas and, if untreated, cancer. The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site; extracolonic adenomas favor the stomach, duodenum, and ampulla. The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site; surveillance targets the stomach, duodenum, and ampulla. The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla of Vater is. The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla of Vater is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without prophylactic colectomy, 100% of FAP patients progress to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: untreated FAP leads to colorectal adenocarcinoma often before age 30 and in nearly all untreated patients by age 50 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 4 — location-dependent presentation of pancreatic head and periampullary tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site; extracoloni","C":"The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site; survei","D":"The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla ","E":"The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site; the ampulla of"}},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man has about 30 colonic adenomas plus several serrated and hyperplastic polyps. His parents have normal screening exams, but his brother has a similar polyp burden. APC testing is negative. Which gene is most likely mutated?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"SMAD4","C":"PTEN","D":"MSH6","E":"MUTYH"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MUTYH-associated polyposis is an autosomal recessive base-excision repair disorder in patients without APC loss. It has fewer than 100 polyps that appear later, with frequent sessile serrated and hyperplastic polyps. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas with serrated polyps. SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis, not recessive adenomatous polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with recessive inheritance. MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with dominant inheritance, not about 30 adenomas in siblings. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MUTYH-associated polyposis is also associated with duodenal adenomas and ovarian and bladder cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancer development in MUTYH-associated polyposis is delayed relative to FAP (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas","B":"SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis,","C":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with re","D":"MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with "}},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with numerous colonic adenomas also has firm bony masses of the skull and a fibrous mass in the abdominal wall. Which variant best describes her disease?","choices":{"A":"Turcot syndrome","B":"Cowden syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Gardner syndrome","E":"Li Fraumeni syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is an APC-related FAP variant with multiple adenomas plus osteomas, thyroid and desmoid tumors, and skin cysts. Turcot syndrome adds CNS tumors such as medulloblastoma. Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC variant with osteomas and desmoids. Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenomas with osteomas and desmoids. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis) (Table 17.10). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turcot syndrome is FAP or Lynch syndrome plus a malignant CNS tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: attenuated FAP is also caused by APC mutation (Robbins Ch. 17, Table 17.10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 5 — hamartomatous polyposis syndromes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC var","C":"Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenom"}},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endometrial carcinoma. Her mother had colon cancer at 45, and tumor staining shows loss of a mismatch repair protein. Which mutational mechanism most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"Chromosomal instability","B":"Telomere attrition crisis","C":"Oncogene amplification","D":"Whole chromosome aneuploidy","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In HNPCC, loss of the second mismatch repair allele raises mutation rates up to 1000-fold, mostly in short repeats called microsatellites. Chromosomal instability is the hallmark of the APC/beta catenin pathway. Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability of the APC pathway, not mismatch repair loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome mismatch repair defects cause subsequent microsatellite instability (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: MSI can target TGFBR2 and BAX, promoting growth and survival of abnormal clones (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability"}},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman with no family history of cancer has a mucinous large bowel adenocarcinoma with peritumoral lymphocytes and mismatch repair deficiency. Which finding best supports a sporadic origin rather than a hereditary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous differentiation","B":"Advanced patient age","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes","D":"BRAF activating mutation","E":"Repair protein loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sporadic mismatch repair–deficient cancers often arise through the CpG island hypermethylation pathway, which silences MLH1. The combination of MSI, BRAF mutation, and methylation is the signature of this pathway. Mucinous change, lymphocytes, and right-sided location occur in both. Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tumors, so it cannot distinguish them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps follow the CIMP pathway, which may silence MLH1 and lead to BRAF mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: KRAS and TP53 are typically intact in CIMP cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tu"}},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman's brother had colon cancer at 38 and her mother had endometrial cancer at 50. Genetic testing confirms a germline mismatch repair mutation. Which other cancer is she at increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian carcinoma","B":"Breast carcinoma","C":"Thyroid carcinoma","D":"Lung carcinoma","E":"Cervical carcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HNPCC clusters cancers of the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin. The objective names endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers. Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3. Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Lynch syndrome mnemonic CEOS stands for colon, endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: identifying germline mismatch repair mutations matters for genetic counseling and second malignancy risk (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch synd"}},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference 12 to 16), MCV is 72 fL (reference 80 to 100), and ferritin is 8 ng/mL (reference 15 to 150). She denies visible bleeding. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy","B":"Colonoscopy referral","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis","D":"Empiric oral iron","E":"Serum B12 level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers often present with fatigue and weakness from iron deficiency anemia. Iron deficiency anemia in an older male or postmenopausal female is GI cancer until proven otherwise. Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already confirms iron deficiency, whose source must be sought by colonoscopy. Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ferritin confirms iron deficiency instead. Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cancer, which iron deficiency in an older woman implies until proven otherwise. B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcytic, low-ferritin anemia points to GI blood loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia in males older than 50 and postmenopausal females raises suspicion for colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: proximal colon tumors grow as polypoid masses that rarely obstruct (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already ","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ","D":"Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cance","E":"B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcy"}},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has months of narrowing stool caliber, crampy left lower quadrant pain, and bloating. Imaging shows a short constricting sigmoid mass. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sessile serrated plaque","B":"Large pedunculated villous polyp","C":"Diffuse linitis plastica","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa","E":"Annular napkin-ring lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distal colon carcinomas tend to be annular napkin-ring constrictions that narrow the lumen, sometimes to obstruction. Left-sided tumors produce visible bleeding, bowel habit change, and left lower quadrant cramping. A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion, not a constricting sigmoid mass. Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, whereas left-sided cancers are annular and constricting. Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or narrowed stools. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: left-sided colorectal cancer is associated with hematochezia and obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: invasive colon cancer elicits a desmoplastic response responsible for its firm consistency (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion,","B":"Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, wherea","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or"}},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man takes daily low-dose aspirin after a myocardial infarction and asks whether it affects his colon cancer risk. Which mechanism most likely explains a protective effect?","choices":{"A":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","B":"Thromboxane receptor blockade","C":"Cyclooxygenase 2 inhibition","D":"Platelet P2Y12 blockade","E":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin and other NSAIDs are protective, likely through inhibition of COX-2, which is highly expressed in 90% of colorectal carcinomas and 40% to 90% of adenomas. COX-2 drives prostaglandin E2, which promotes epithelial proliferation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: overexpression of COX-2 has been linked to colorectal cancer, and NSAIDs may be chemopreventive (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some NSAIDs cause polyp regression in FAP patients whose rectum was left in place (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs and H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has periumbilical pain that moved to the right lower quadrant, then nausea and low-grade fever. His resected appendix is examined. Which histologic finding is required for the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria neutrophils","B":"Mucosal neutrophils with ulcers","C":"Lymphoid follicle hyperplasia","D":"Submucosal eosinophil clusters","E":"Granulomas in lamina propria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are not specific. Diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria. Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young patients, but it is a cause rather than the diagnostic finding. Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or infection, not acute appendicitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can be due to fecalith obstruction in adults or lymphoid hyperplasia in children (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, abscess, and bacteremia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young pati","E":"Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or in"}},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic constipation and straining notices bright red blood on toilet tissue. Anoscopy shows dilated anal venous cushions. Which condition must still be excluded?","choices":{"A":"Anal fistula","B":"Pilonidal cyst","C":"Colon carcinoma","D":"Perianal abscess","E":"Rectal prolapse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hemorrhoids present with pain and bright red blood on toilet tissue, but colon cancer can coexist and must be excluded. Straining at defecation and venous stasis of pregnancy are the main predisposing influences. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hemorrhoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids receive visceral innervation and are not painful (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: hemorrhoidal bleeding can be treated by sclerotherapy, rubber band ligation, or infrared coagulation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has progressive abdominal distention. Surgery finds tenacious, semisolid mucin filling the peritoneal cavity and a ruptured, mucin-distended appendix with pushing tumor margins. Which primary lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Appendiceal carcinoid tumor","B":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasm","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma","E":"Gastric signet ring cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasms occur in adults, most often in the sixth decade, with pushing margins and extracellular mucin that can rupture the appendix. Peritoneal spread causes pseudomyxoma peritonei. An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rarely spreads, not a mucin-distended ruptured appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal spread of appendiceal mucinous neoplasms can produce pseudomyxoma peritonei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: GNAS, encoding Gαs, is frequently mutated in appendiceal mucinous neoplasms and is thought to drive excess mucin production (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — mucinous pancreatic cystic neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rare"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has a 6 mm pedunculated polyp on screening endoscopy that proves to be a tubular adenoma. Which gross texture is typical of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Velvety raspberry-like surface","B":"Smooth glistening dome","C":"Umbilicated central pit","D":"Firm rubbery nodule","E":"Translucent cystic bleb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colorectal adenomas can be pedunculated or sessile, and both have a surface texture resembling velvet or a raspberry. Pedunculated adenomas have slender fibromuscular stalks with submucosal vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic polyps may be flat, sessile, or pedunculated (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: the stalk of a pedunculated adenoma is usually covered by nonneoplastic epithelium (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl with a known germline APC mutation has random biopsies of normal-appearing colonic mucosa. They show isolated foci of one or two dysplastic crypts. What are these foci?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyps","B":"Inflammatory polyps","C":"Hamartomatous polyps","D":"Microscopic adenomas","E":"Lymphoid aggregates"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In FAP, flat or depressed adenomas are prevalent, and microscopic adenomas of one or two dysplastic crypts occur in otherwise normal-appearing mucosa. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP follows the two-hit hypothesis for the APC tumor suppressor gene (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: FAP patients develop numerous colorectal adenomas as teenagers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man carries a germline mismatch repair gene mutation, and his colon looks unremarkable. Which event in a single crypt cell is needed before mismatch repair fails?","choices":{"A":"Gain of oncogene copies","B":"Paternal gene imprinting","C":"Mitochondrial DNA depletion","D":"Telomerase gene silencing","E":"Loss of the normal allele"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HNPCC patients inherit one mutant gene and one normal allele. When the second copy is lost through mutation or epigenetic silencing, mismatch repair fails and mutations accumulate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: HNPCC patients develop cancer at younger ages than sporadic colon cancer patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a cecal adenocarcinoma found during evaluation for fatigue. How does a tumor at this site typically grow?","choices":{"A":"Linitis-like infiltration","B":"Diffuse wall thickening","C":"Polypoid exophytic mass","D":"Flat ulcerated plaque","E":"Submucosal cystic mass"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Proximal colon tumors often grow as polypoid, exophytic masses along one wall of the large-caliber cecum and ascending colon, and they rarely cause obstruction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ascending colon cancer presents as an exophytic mass with weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some poorly differentiated colorectal carcinomas form few glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man has a perforated appendix removed. A week later he has spiking fevers, right upper quadrant pain, and septic thrombosis of the portal vein. Which complication has occurred?","choices":{"A":"Ascending cholangitis","B":"Hepatic sarcoidosis","C":"Viral hepatitis","D":"Pylephlebitis","E":"Subphrenic hematoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, liver abscess, and bacteremia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can perforate and cause peritonitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: surgeons accept removing some normal appendices rather than delaying resection of a diseased one (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a resected sigmoid adenocarcinoma. He asks which tumor features best predict his prognosis. Which is one of the two most important?","choices":{"A":"Histologic tumor grade","B":"Depth of invasion","C":"Age at diagnosis","D":"Tumor location","E":"Mucin production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The two most important prognostic factors are depth of invasion and lymph node metastases. Poorly differentiated and mucinous histology are associated with worse prognosis but matter less. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer screening alternatives include FIT, FIT-fecal DNA, and CT colonography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the overall 5-year survival for colorectal cancer in the United States is 65% (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true}],"34":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most severely in kwashiorkor?","choices":{"A":"Somatic protein compartment","B":"Bone mineral compartment","C":"Plasma lipid compartment","D":"Intracellular water compartment","E":"Visceral protein compartment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor results when protein deprivation is relatively greater than the reduction in total calories, and it causes marked loss of the visceral protein compartment, chiefly the liver, with resulting hypoalbuminemia. Marasmus predominantly depletes the somatic compartment of skeletal muscle. Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor, which depletes visceral protein, chiefly the liver. Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment; kwashiorkor depletes visceral protein stores. Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment; kwashiorkor instead causes edema from visceral protein loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Protein Energy Malnutrition). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kwashiorkor mnemonic MEALS lists malnutrition, edema, anemia, liver malfunction, and skin lesions (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: serum albumin and transferrin reflect the visceral protein compartment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor,","C":"Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment; kwashiorkor deple","D":"Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment; kwashio"}},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Marasmus is defined when a child's weight falls to about what percentage of normal for sex, height, and age?","choices":{"A":"About 30 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 45 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins defines marasmus as weight falling to about 60% of normal for sex, height, and age, with growth retardation and loss of muscle and subcutaneous fat. In kwashiorkor, weight is typically 60% to 80% of normal, and the true loss is masked by edema. About 30 percent is far below the threshold; marasmus is defined at about 60% of normal weight. About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60% of normal weight. About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% to 80%, not the marasmus threshold. About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define marasmus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of edema distinguishes marasmus from kwashiorkor (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: in marasmus the head appears large relative to the emaciated body (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"About 30 percent is far below the threshold; marasmus is def","C":"About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60","D":"About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% t","E":"About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define m"}},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"The enlarged fatty liver in kwashiorkor results mainly from which process?","choices":{"A":"Increased delivery of dietary fat","B":"Hepatic venous congestion","C":"Hepatocyte iron accumulation","D":"Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis","E":"Excess hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In kwashiorkor the liver is enlarged and fatty because synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins is reduced, so triglyceride is not exported efficiently. The diet is typically carbohydrate based rather than fat rich. Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes from failure to make lipoprotein carrier proteins. Excess glycogen is not the problem; triglyceride accumulates because apolipoprotein synthesis falls. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes the fatty change in kwashiorkor to decreased apolipoprotein synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: superimposed fatty change can occur in marasmus as well, but it is characteristic of kwashiorkor (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes fr","E":"Excess glycogen is not the problem; triglyceride accumulates"}},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Acute vitamin A toxicity produces headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that can mimic which condition?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Pseudotumor cerebri","C":"Migraine with aura","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute toxicity from a large dose of vitamin A causes headache, dizziness, vomiting, stupor, and blurred vision, symptoms that may be confused with a brain tumor, known as pseudotumor cerebri. Chronic toxicity causes weight loss, anorexia, nausea, and bone and joint pain. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Toxicity). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin A excess causes idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and isotretinoin is teratogenic (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: retinoic acid stimulates osteoclast production and activity, so chronic excess raises fracture risk (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin K is required for hepatic carboxylation of which group of clotting factors?","choices":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII","B":"Factors VIII, IX, XI, and XII","C":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X","D":"Factors II, V, VIII, and X","E":"Factors III, VII, XI, and XIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of procoagulant factors II, VII, IX, and X and of proteins C and S. Deficiency produces a bleeding diathesis, especially in fat malabsorption and after broad antibiotic courses. Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; the dependent factors are II, VII, IX, and X. Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only IX of this set is. Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set is wrong despite including II and X. Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only VII of this set is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase and lowers functional factor levels (First Aid Hematology, Warfarin). COMLEX: vitamin K is fat soluble, so biliary and pancreatic disease reduce its absorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; th","B":"Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only ","D":"Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set ","E":"Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only "}},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor stimulates renal 1-alpha hydroxylase to increase active vitamin D production?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Fibroblast growth factor 23","C":"Serum hypercalcemia","D":"Serum hyperphosphatemia","E":"Calcitriol excess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After hepatic 25-hydroxylation, the kidney converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and hypophosphatemia and inhibited by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and FGF23. Hypocalcemia acts indirectly by raising PTH. Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxylase; it is hypocalcemia that raises PTH. Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophosphatemia does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Metabolism). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease reduces renal activation of vitamin D (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the major circulating form and the usual marker of vitamin D status (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxyla","D":"Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophospha"}},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Riboflavin functions in the body after conversion to which coenzyme?","choices":{"A":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate","D":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Riboflavin is converted to flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide, which serve as cofactors for many enzymes in intermediary metabolism. Deficiency causes cheilosis, stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis, and corneal vascularization. NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not riboflavin. Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not riboflavin. Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization, the two Cs of B2 (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B2). COMLEX: niacin forms the nicotinamide coenzymes, while thiamine forms the pyrophosphate (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin.","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not ri","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin."}},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Copper is an essential component of which enzyme needed for collagen cross-linking?","choices":{"A":"Lysyl oxidase","B":"Glutathione peroxidase","C":"Carbonic anhydrase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Copper is a component of cytochrome c oxidase, dopamine beta hydroxylase, tyrosinase, lysyl oxidase, and other enzymes. Deficiency causes muscle weakness, neurologic defects, and abnormal collagen cross-linking, and it may follow excess zinc intake. Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant enzyme, not the copper enzyme of collagen cross-linking. Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent lysyl oxidase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menkes disease impairs copper transport and lysyl oxidase activity (First Aid Biochemistry, Menkes disease). COMLEX: glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not copper (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant ","C":"Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent ly"}},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Selenium deficiency is the cause of which endemic cardiomyopathy?","choices":{"A":"Wet beriberi","B":"Chagas disease","C":"Keshan disease","D":"Kawasaki disease","E":"Loeffler endocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Selenium is a component of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme. Deficiency causes myopathy and congestive cardiomyopathy known as Keshan disease, described in regions with selenium-poor soil. Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine deficiency, not selenium deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutathione peroxidase is a free radical scavenging enzyme (First Aid Pathology, Free radical injury). COMLEX: the antioxidant role of selenium overlaps functionally with vitamin E (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine defi"}},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"The central processing system that integrates peripheral signals of energy balance lies mainly in which hypothalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Supraoptic nucleus","B":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"Preoptic nucleus","D":"Arcuate nucleus","E":"Mammillary nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes energy balance as an afferent system, a central processing system in the hypothalamus, and an efferent system. Within the arcuate nucleus, POMC and CART neurons promote catabolism and NPY and AgRP neurons promote anabolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Regulation of Energy Balance). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a ventromedial hypothalamic lesion causes hyperphagia and obesity (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: arcuate NPY and AgRP neurons also inhibit neighboring POMC neurons (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Peptide YY and glucagon-like peptide 1 are secreted by which intestinal cell type?","choices":{"A":"K cells","B":"I cells","C":"S cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptide YY and GLP-1 are secreted by L cells in the distal ileum and colon. GIP comes from K cells in the duodenum and jejunum. These gut signals act on the hypothalamus and pancreas after a meal. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are the incretin hormones of the small intestine (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Gastrointestinal regulatory substances). COMLEX: incretins act on pancreatic beta cells after enteral glucose (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Binding of GLP-1 to its receptor on pancreatic beta cells increases which second messenger to promote insulin release?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incretin receptors on beta cells are G protein coupled receptors that activate adenylate cyclase. The resulting rise in cyclic AMP enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the incretin effect explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose (First Aid Endocrine, Insulin). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 are the two principal incretins (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Gallstones are about how many times more common in obese individuals than in lean individuals?","choices":{"A":"About two times","B":"About six times","C":"About three times","D":"About ten times","E":"About twenty times"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallstones are six times more common in obese than in lean individuals. Obesity increases total body cholesterol turnover and biliary cholesterol excretion, which promotes cholesterol stone formation. Two times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obese individuals. Three times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obesity. Ten times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obesity. Twenty times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six times more common in obesity. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Gallbladder Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol stones are linked to obesity, female sex, and estrogen (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Cholelithiasis). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol supersaturation is the key event in cholesterol stones (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about six ","C":"Three times underestimates the risk; gallstones are about si","D":"Ten times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six time","E":"Twenty times overstates the risk; gallstones are about six t"}},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity is associated with about what share of all cancers diagnosed in the United States?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 70 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that about 40% of all cancers diagnosed in the United States are associated with being overweight or obese. Gastrointestinal sites include the esophagus, upper stomach, colorectum, gallbladder, liver, and pancreas. About 5 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. About 15 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. About 70 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. About 90 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts for about 40% of US cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obesity raises esophageal adenocarcinoma risk through reflux and Barrett esophagus (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Esophageal cancer). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia and adipokine changes link obesity to cancer (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 5 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts f","B":"About 15 percent understates obesity's role, which accounts ","D":"About 70 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts f","E":"About 90 percent overstates obesity's role, which accounts f"}},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity raises free estrogen in part by reducing hepatic synthesis of which protein?","choices":{"A":"Thyroxine binding globulin","B":"Corticosteroid binding globulin","C":"Vitamin D binding protein","D":"Retinol binding protein","E":"Sex hormone binding globulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Obesity increases estrogen synthesis from androgens in adipose tissue and reduces hepatic synthesis of sex hormone binding globulin, raising free estrogen. This mechanism helps explain the higher risk of endometrial and breast cancer. Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and does not regulate free estrogen. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aromatase in adipose tissue converts androgens to estrogen (First Aid Reproductive, Estrogen). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia also suppresses sex hormone binding globulin (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and"}},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy in a refugee camp was weaned after his sister was born and now eats mainly maize gruel. He is apathetic and has generalized pitting edema, peeling hyperpigmented skin patches, pale banded hair, and hepatomegaly. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which change most directly produces his edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","B":"Increased endothelial permeability","C":"Obstructed lymphatic drainage","D":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This child has kwashiorkor. Loss of visceral protein lowers serum albumin, which reduces plasma oncotic pressure and allows fluid to leave capillaries, producing generalized edema. The flaky paint dermatosis and banded hair are characteristic accompanying findings. Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor edema results from hypoalbuminemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a small child with a swollen belly and peeling skin is the classic kwashiorkor picture (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: kwashiorkor is also seen with protein-losing enteropathy, nephrotic syndrome, and burns (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor "}},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had little food of any kind for months. She is emaciated with wasted limbs, no subcutaneous fat, and a head that looks large for her body. There is no edema. Her weight is 55% of expected for age. Which hormonal change most likely contributes to the loss of her fat stores?","choices":{"A":"Increased cortisol secretion","B":"Increased leptin secretion","C":"Increased insulin secretion","D":"Decreased glucagon secretion","E":"Decreased epinephrine secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This child has marasmus. Low leptin levels stimulate the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and the resulting high cortisol contributes to lipolysis. Fat and muscle are mobilized as energy sources. Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, and low leptin drives the cortisol rise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: marasmus results from overall calorie deficiency rather than isolated protein lack (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus also causes immune deficiency, especially of T cell mediated immunity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, a"}},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy is treated for protein malnutrition with generalized edema, skin desquamation, and a fatty liver. After starting full-strength milk-based feeds he develops bloating and watery diarrhea. Stool is acidic and positive for reducing substances. Which mechanism most likely explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency","B":"Mucosal disaccharidase loss","C":"Cow milk protein allergy","D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, small bowel crypt mitoses decrease, causing mucosal atrophy, loss of villi and microvilli, and secondary disaccharidase deficiency. Infants may therefore respond poorly to full-strength milk diets until the mucosa recovers. Acidic stool with reducing substances points to undigested sugar. Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool with reducing substances, which marks undigested sugar. Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea, not acidic stool with reducing substances after milk feeds. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactase deficiency gives osmotic diarrhea with low stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the mucosal changes of kwashiorkor reverse with treatment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea, n"}},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 120 cm of distal ileum resected 3 years ago. She has greasy stools and now has trouble seeing when driving at dusk. Examination shows dry conjunctivae with small gray foamy plaques. The deficient vitamin normally serves which function?","choices":{"A":"Cofactor for gamma carboxylation","B":"Membrane lipid antioxidant","C":"Regulator of calcium absorption","D":"Component of visual pigment","E":"Cofactor for collagen hydroxylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin A deficiency develops in fat malabsorption such as Crohn disease because absorption requires bile and lipid digestion. Retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin, so night blindness appears first, followed by xerophthalmia and Bitot spots. Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness. Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficiency causes ataxia and hemolysis, not xerophthalmia. Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency causes osteomalacia, not night blindness. Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency causes scurvy, not Bitot spots. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Deficiency). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bitot spots and night blindness indicate vitamin A deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: vitamin A deficiency also causes squamous metaplasia of airway and urinary epithelium (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — terminal ileal disease and bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficien","B":"Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficien","C":"Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency","E":"Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency cau"}},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis rarely takes her pancreatic enzymes. She has a wide-based gait, absent ankle reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with MCV 88 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Serum methylmalonic acid is normal. Which process most likely underlies her findings?","choices":{"A":"Myelin methylation failure","B":"Clotting factor undercarboxylation","C":"Membrane lipid peroxidation","D":"Collagen hydroxylation failure","E":"Rhodopsin regeneration failure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant that protects membrane lipids from free radical peroxidation. Deficiency, seen with fat malabsorption such as pancreatic insufficiency, causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. A normal methylmalonic acid argues against B12 deficiency. Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not spinocerebellar degeneration. Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleeding gums and petechiae, not ataxia. Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which causes night blindness, not ataxia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin E). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin E deficiency mimics B12 neuropathy without megaloblastosis or raised methylmalonic acid (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin E). COMLEX: vitamin E prevents oxidation of unsaturated fats in cell membranes (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitam","D":"Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleedi","E":"Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which"}},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with poorly controlled celiac disease has diffuse bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Serum calcium is 8.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphorus 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L), and PTH 98 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). A bone biopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization","B":"Mosaic pattern of lamellar bone","C":"Marrow fibrosis with brown tumors","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae","E":"Thickened seams of unmineralized osteoid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D malabsorption leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which restores calcium toward normal but leaves phosphate low. Impaired mineralization in the adult produces osteomalacia, in which excess unmineralized osteoid accumulates on bone surfaces. Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteoporosis, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Deficiency States). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteomalacia shows low calcium or phosphate with high PTH and alkaline phosphatase (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Osteomalacia). COMLEX: the same defect in children causes rickets with growth plate deformities (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteopor"}},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted with confusion and receives an IV dextrose infusion. Hours later he has nystagmus, bilateral lateral gaze palsy, and a wide-based gait. Serum lactate is 42 mg/dL (reference range: 4.5–19.8 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most directly explains his lactate rise?","choices":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase","C":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","D":"Fructose bisphosphatase","E":"Phosphofructokinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the coenzyme for oxidative decarboxylation reactions, including pyruvate dehydrogenase. Without it, pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and a glucose load worsens the deficit. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion indicate Wernicke encephalopathy. Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually forms the excess lactate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Thiamine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: give thiamine before dextrose in alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B1). COMLEX: thiamine deficiency also causes dry and wet beriberi (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually"}},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a metastatic midgut neuroendocrine tumor with episodic flushing and diarrhea. Over months she develops a rough dermatitis on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms and new forgetfulness. Diversion of which amino acid most likely explains her new findings?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine","B":"Histidine","C":"Methionine","D":"Phenylalanine","E":"Tryptophan"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan. Carcinoid tumors divert tryptophan toward serotonin, reducing niacin synthesis and producing pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Sources: First Aid Biochemistry (Vitamin B3: niacin from tryptophan; carcinoid syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pellagra occurs with carcinoid syndrome, Hartnup disease, and isoniazid use (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B3). COMLEX: niacin forms NAD and NADP for redox reactions (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has received home parenteral nutrition for 6 months using a trace element mix that omitted zinc, with other trace elements supplied after extensive small bowel resection. He now has a red scaly rash around his mouth, nose, and anus, worsening diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste. Which additional finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired night vision","B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zinc deficiency from zinc-free parenteral nutrition causes a periorificial rash like acrodermatitis enteropathica, anorexia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and impaired night vision. Zinc is a component of many enzymes, principally oxidases. Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), not zinc deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zinc deficiency causes delayed wound healing, dysgeusia, and hypogonadism (First Aid Biochemistry, Zinc). COMLEX: acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form of zinc malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, no","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not z","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not ","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan"}},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has had insatiable hunger and severe obesity since infancy. Serum leptin is undetectable, and sequencing shows a homozygous mutation in the leptin gene. She begins daily recombinant leptin injections. The expected decrease in her food intake is mediated mainly by which hypothalamic effect?","choices":{"A":"Activation of NPY and AgRP neurons","B":"Activation of lateral orexin neurons","C":"Activation of lateral MCH neurons","D":"Inhibition of paraventricular TRH neurons","E":"Activation of POMC and CART neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin acts in the arcuate nucleus to stimulate POMC and CART neurons, which release alpha MSH to act on melanocortin receptors and decrease food intake. It also inhibits NPY and AgRP neurons that drive feeding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin is secreted by adipose tissue and decreases appetite (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: mutations of leptin or its receptor cause massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 undergoes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Six months later she reports much less hunger before meals than she had with prior diets. A blunted rise of which hormone most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Cholecystokinin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is an orexigenic gut hormone that increases food intake. It is produced mainly in the stomach, rises before meals, and acts on NPY and AgRP neurons. Its preprandial rise is blunted after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, which may contribute to weight loss. Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appetite, not the premeal hunger hormone blunted by bypass. Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blunted rise would not lessen hunger. Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would not explain reduced hunger. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome and with sleep deprivation (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: ghrelin levels are typically lower in obese individuals (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appeti","B":"Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blun","D":"Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would"}},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 34 starts a weekly injected drug that mimics a hormone of ileal and colonic endocrine cells. Over 6 months his hemoglobin A1c improves and he loses 9 kg. Which action of the drug most likely accounts for his weight loss?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption","B":"Central suppression of food intake","C":"Increased brown fat thermogenesis","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion","E":"Decreased hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GLP-1 from intestinal L cells is an incretin that augments insulin release, and it also acts on the hypothalamus to decrease food intake and slows gastric emptying. GLP-1 receptor agonists produce weight loss mainly through reduced appetite. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Costanzo Ch. 8 (Hunger and Satiety).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonists decrease appetite and slow gastric emptying (First Aid Endocrine, Diabetes mellitus therapy). COMLEX: lipase inhibitors reduce fat absorption, a different weight loss mechanism (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 37 has acanthosis nigricans and a waist circumference of 118 cm. Fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL), and HDL 32 mg/dL (reference range: 40–60 mg/dL). Which mediator, released after inflammasome activation by excess free fatty acids, most likely drives his insulin resistance?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 10","B":"Interleukin 4","C":"Transforming growth factor beta","D":"Interleukin 1","E":"Interferon alpha"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obesity, excess free fatty acids and lipid within cells activate the inflammasome, which releases interleukin 1 and creates a systemic proinflammatory state. This inflammation promotes insulin resistance, a core feature of the metabolic syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Metabolic Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic syndrome combines central obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia (First Aid Endocrine, Metabolic syndrome). COMLEX: obesity is associated with high triglycerides and low HDL (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with a BMI of 38 and type 2 diabetes drinks alcohol rarely. ALT is 78 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L) and AST is 52 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L). Ultrasound shows a diffusely echogenic liver, and viral and autoimmune studies are negative. A reduced serum level of which adipose hormone most likely contributes to her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Adiponectin","B":"Leptin","C":"Resistin","D":"Angiotensinogen","E":"Testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease. Adiponectin, which is reduced in obesity, increases fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity in liver and muscle. Its decline contributes to insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and hepatic steatosis. Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of central leptin resistance. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Adiponectin; Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASLD is the hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome, with ALT usually above AST (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity related steatosis may progress to steatohepatitis and fibrosis (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of centr"}},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a BMI of 39 and fasting hyperinsulinemia is diagnosed with sigmoid adenocarcinoma. His physician explains that high insulin raises free levels of a growth factor that stimulates tumor cell proliferation. Through which signaling route does this growth factor mainly act?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and catenin pathways","B":"RAS and PI3K pathways","C":"Notch and Hedgehog pathways","D":"TGF beta and SMAD pathways","E":"JAK and STAT pathways"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperinsulinemia raises free insulin-like growth factor 1, a mitogen whose receptor activates the RAS and PI3K AKT pathways and promotes growth of tumor cells. This is one proposed link between obesity and colorectal and other cancers. Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the target of insulin-like growth factor 1. TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway lost in colon cancer, not the IGF-1 mitogenic route. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include obesity, red meat, and a low fiber diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: excess adipose estrogen production is another obesity cancer link (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma risk factors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the t","D":"TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway l"}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-month-old girl in the United States has been fed only a rice beverage since weaning. She has generalized edema, flaky skin, and a fatty liver. Which tissue is relatively spared?","choices":{"A":"Subcutaneous fat stores","B":"Hepatic protein stores","C":"Plasma albumin pool","D":"Thymic lymphoid tissue","E":"Intestinal villous mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, the visceral compartment is markedly depleted while loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle mass is relatively mild and masked by edema. Thymic and lymphoid atrophy and small bowel villous loss are prominent. Robbins notes cases in developed countries from milk replacement with rice-based beverages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kwashiorkor follows a diet deficient in protein despite adequate calories (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus depletes subcutaneous fat and muscle more severely (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has taken no food by mouth for 2 weeks while receiving broad-spectrum antibiotics. He now oozes from IV sites. PT is 24 s (reference range: 11–15 s). He takes no anticoagulants. What is the main cause?","choices":{"A":"Increased hepatic vitamin breakdown","B":"Increased renal vitamin loss","C":"Blocked epoxide reductase activity","D":"Loss of gut bacterial synthesis","E":"Impaired platelet granule release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin K comes from the diet and from intestinal bacteria. Without oral intake and with gut flora suppressed by antibiotics, vitamin K dependent factors fall and the PT rises. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Vitamin K).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin K deficiency prolongs PT and aPTT with a normal bleeding time (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin K). COMLEX: vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of clotting factors (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old widower eats mostly tea and toast. He has bleeding gums, corkscrew hairs, and perifollicular hemorrhages. The deficient vitamin is a cofactor for which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Gamma glutamyl carboxylase","B":"Prolyl hydroxylase","C":"Pyruvate carboxylase","D":"Glutathione peroxidase","E":"Transketolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin C activates prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases needed for collagen hydroxylation. Deficiency, seen in older adults with poor diets, causes scurvy with gum bleeding and perifollicular hemorrhages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin C). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scurvy causes swollen gums, bruising, and corkscrew hair (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin C). COMLEX: vitamin C excess can cause oxalate stones and iron overload (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with a BMI of 41 has a high serum leptin level. She remains hungry despite large fat stores. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic leptin resistance","B":"Rapid renal leptin clearance","C":"Circulating antileptin antibodies","D":"Reduced adipocyte leptin release","E":"Excess hepatic leptin uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin is secreted in proportion to fat stores, so obese people usually have high leptin. Most are resistant to its central effects, so the satiety signal fails despite abundant hormone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin acts on the ventromedial hypothalamus to promote satiety (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: leptin gene mutations are a rare cause of massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes has steatotic liver disease on biopsy without fibrosis. He drinks no alcohol. Without weight loss, which complication is he at risk for?","choices":{"A":"Budd-Chiari syndrome","B":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","C":"Hepatic cirrhosis","D":"Biliary cholangitis","E":"Autoimmune hepatitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Steatotic liver disease linked to obesity and metabolic syndrome can progress to steatohepatitis, fibrosis, and cirrhosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASH can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity is a major cause of steatotic liver disease (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 10-month-old breastfed infant living in a northern city has soft skull bones and bowed legs. Which chest finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sternal pectus excavatum","B":"Costochondral beading","C":"Clavicular callus formation","D":"Inferior rib notching","E":"Scapular winging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin D deficiency in children causes rickets. Overgrowth of cartilage at costochondral junctions produces the rachitic rosary, along with craniotabes and leg bowing. Breastfed infants with little sun exposure are at risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Rickets). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rickets shows rachitic rosary, bowed legs, and craniotabes (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: the chest may also show a pigeon breast deformity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"}],"35":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do Strongyloides stercoralis larvae enter the human host?","choices":{"A":"Penetration of intact skin","B":"Ingestion of eggs in soil","C":"Bite of a sandfly vector","D":"Inhalation of soil dust","E":"Ingestion of raw fish meat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Strongyloides worms live in soil, and humans are infected when larvae penetrate the skin. The larvae travel in the circulation to the lungs, move up the trachea, and are swallowed. Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinworm are acquired, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin. Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not Strongyloides. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides, Ancylostoma, and Necator enter through the skin, while Enterobius, Ascaris, Toxocara, Trichinella, and Trichuris are ingested (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides occurs sporadically in the United States, including in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinw","E":"Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not "}},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do humans acquire Ascaris lumbricoides?","choices":{"A":"Ingestion of metacercariae on plants","B":"Ingestion of eggs passed in stool","C":"Ingestion of cysts in pond water","D":"Bites of infected deer flies","E":"Inhalation of larvae in soil dust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs from soil contaminated with human stool (A. lumbricoides) or pig stool (A. suum). Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as Fasciola, not Ascaris. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris is spread fecal-orally, and its knobby-coated oval eggs are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: about a billion people are estimated to be infected with Ascaris (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as "}},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"Humans acquire the adult intestinal form of Taenia solium by ingesting what?","choices":{"A":"Eggs in fecally soiled water","B":"Metacercariae on water plants","C":"Cysticerci in undercooked pork","D":"Cercariae in fresh lake water","E":"Eggs in undercooked raw beef meat"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ingested cysticerci in undercooked pork attach to the intestinal wall and develop into adult tapeworms several meters long. This route completes the life cycle without causing disseminated cysticercosis. Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not the adult intestinal tapeworm. Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia solium. Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomiasis, not taeniasis. Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. solium, and eggs are not the infective form in meat. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium intestinal tapeworm comes from encysted larvae in pork, and cysticercosis comes from eggs in food contaminated with human feces (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: tapeworm proglottids contain both male and female reproductive organs (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not ","B":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia so","D":"Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomi","E":"Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. so"}},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"For most intestinal helminths, which factor determines the adult worm burden?","choices":{"A":"Number of infective exposures","B":"Rate of adult worm replication","C":"Host gastric acid output","D":"Duration of fecal egg shedding","E":"Peak blood eosinophil count"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most intestinal helminths do not multiply to new adults inside the human host, so each adult worm reflects a separately acquired infective egg or larva. Strongyloides is the key exception. Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden depends on the number of exposures. Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine it, since eggs must mature outside the host. Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of adult worms. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides hyperinfection reflects accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris symptoms vary with the burden of worms, and light infections are often asymptomatic (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden de","D":"Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine","E":"Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of"}},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"In strongyloidiasis, what allows the worm burden to rise without new exposure from soil?","choices":{"A":"Adult worms dividing by fission","B":"Eggs hatching in moist soil","C":"Larvae maturing within the host","D":"Cysts forming in skeletal muscle","E":"Proglottids budding off the scolex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most Strongyloides larvae pass in stool, but some develop into infective forms within the host, leading to autoinfection. Females in the small intestinal mucosa produce eggs by parthenogenesis. Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burden rises through autoinfection by larvae maturing in the host. Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly what autoinfection bypasses. Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongyloides autoinfection. Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform Strongyloides larvae are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: immunocompetent hosts with Strongyloides are often asymptomatic but may have diarrhea, bloating, or malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burd","B":"Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly","D":"Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongylo","E":"Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not St"}},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class both suppresses eosinophil function and stimulates female Strongyloides to produce more infective larvae?","choices":{"A":"Beta blockers","B":"Antihistamines","C":"Statin agents","D":"Loop diuretic agents","E":"Corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils and other immune cells that accumulate in response to infection, and they stimulate female worms to increase larva production. Prolonged therapy can lead to fatal hyperinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ivermectin or bendazoles treat Strongyloides (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: organ transplantation and lymphoma also increase the risk of hyperinfection (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"How do hookworms cause anemia?","choices":{"A":"Hemolysis of circulating red cells","B":"Blood loss from the intestinal wall","C":"Marrow invasion by migrating larvae","D":"Competition for dietary cobalamin","E":"Malabsorption of dietary folate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ancylostoma and Necator attach to the intestinal wall and suck blood, causing chronic blood loss and microcytic anemia. Hookworms do not hemolyze red cells; they suck blood from the intestinal wall, causing iron deficiency. Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothrium latum, not hookworm. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia is the classic parasite hint for Ancylostoma and Necator (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae penetrate skin after walking barefoot on contaminated soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hookworms do not hemolyze red cells; they suck blood from th","D":"Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothri"}},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nutrient deficiency results from Diphyllobothrium latum infection?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Folic acid","C":"Thiamine","D":"Vitamin B12","E":"Ascorbic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia. Robbins notes that in humans it lives only in the gut and does not form cysticerci. Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium; it competes for vitamin B12. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diphyllobothrium latum is acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the beef tapeworm and fish tapeworm are acquired by eating undercooked meat or fish (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium; it competes for "}},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can mechanically obstruct which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal valve","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Lower esophageal sphincter","D":"Internal anal sphincter","E":"Duodenojejunal flexure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can cause abdominal obstruction requiring surgery, including obstruction of the ileocecal valve and the appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may also cause biliary obstruction and intestinal perforation (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult Ascaris worms are 15 to 35 cm long and live mainly in the jejunum (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which Th2 cytokine stimulates eosinophils in helminth infections?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 2","B":"Interleukin 12","C":"Interleukin 17","D":"Interferon gamma","E":"Interleukin 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Invasive helminths stimulate a Th2 response. IL-5 stimulates eosinophils, and IL-4 drives IgE production. IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response to helminths. Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit Th2 responses with IL-5. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Filariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils produce major basic protein and respond to IL-5 (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: Th2 responses increase intestinal contractility, which expels adult Trichinella worms in animal models (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response ","D":"Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit"}},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"When is peripheral eosinophilia most expected during a helminth infection?","choices":{"A":"When adults reach peak fecundity","B":"When eggs pass in the stool","C":"When worms attach by a scolex","D":"When larvae invade host tissues","E":"When adults reside in the cecum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eosinophilia is driven by Th2 responses to parasites that migrate through or encyst in tissue. Trichinella larvae invading muscle cause marked eosinophilia, and Ascaris larvae in the lungs cause eosinophilic pneumonitis. Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia, which requires tissue invasion. Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause systemic eosinophilia. Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal tapeworms do not cause eosinophilia. Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not cause eosinophilia. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites lead the PACMAN mnemonic for causes of eosinophilia (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: granulomas around schistosome eggs contain eosinophils, which are distinctive for helminth infections (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia","B":"Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause sys","C":"Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal ","E":"Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not "}},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which eosinophil granule product is toxic to helminths?","choices":{"A":"Cathelicidin peptide","B":"Major basic protein","C":"Serum amyloid A","D":"Lysozyme enzyme","E":"Lactoferrin protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein, a helminthotoxin, along with eosinophil cationic protein and eosinophil-derived neurotoxin. Sources: First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils have bilobate nuclei and large uniform eosinophilic granules (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils that accumulate in tissues in response to Strongyloides (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q25","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"How does pyrantel pamoate eliminate susceptible intestinal nematodes?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of tubulin polymerization","B":"Increased tegument calcium permeability","C":"Neuromuscular blockade of worms","D":"Inhibition of parasite folate synthesis","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pyrantel is a poorly absorbed neuromuscular blocking agent that releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, paralyzing luminal worms, which are then expelled. Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimidazoles such as albendazole, not pyrantel. Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of praziquantel, not pyrantel. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Pyrantel Pamoate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrantel pamoate is listed with ivermectin, mebendazole, and diethylcarbamazine as antihelminthic therapy (First Aid Microbiology (Antihelminthic therapy)). COMLEX: pyrantel is ineffective against Trichuris and Strongyloides infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimida","B":"Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of pr"}},{"id":"sdl35_q26","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"What shape do Enterobius vermicularis eggs have on microscopic examination?","choices":{"A":"Barrel shape with polar plugs","B":"Oval with one flattened side","C":"Round with a thick knobby shell","D":"Oval with a large lateral spine","E":"Operculated with a terminal knob"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm eggs have a characteristic elongated oval shape with one of the long sides of the oval flattened. Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinworm. Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascaris, not pinworm. Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni, not pinworm. Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm causes anal pruritus worse at night and is most common in children aged 5 to 10 (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult pinworms live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinwo","C":"Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascar","D":"Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni","E":"Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, "}},{"id":"sdl35_q27","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice treats intestinal infection with the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Diethylcarbamazine","D":"Pyrantel pamoate","E":"Mebendazole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung lists praziquantel or niclosamide as the drugs of choice for Diphyllobothrium latum and Taenia saginata, which live only in the gut lumen. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca, not tapeworms. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestinal tapeworms. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not tapeworms. Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms, whereas praziquantel is the drug of choice for tapeworms. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tapeworms such as Diphyllobothrium latum are covered with the cestodes (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: a single 2-g dose of niclosamide cures more than 85% of Diphyllobothrium latum infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Oncho","C":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestina","D":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, A","E":"Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms, whereas"}},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old pig farmer passes a 25-cm worm in his stool. He recalls a dry cough with fever several weeks earlier. After the eggs were ingested, which route did the larvae take before maturing in his intestine?","choices":{"A":"Portal flow to the hepatic lobules","B":"Lymphatic flow to the dermis","C":"Direct growth in the jejunal lumen","D":"Lung passage then reswallowing","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ingested Ascaris eggs release larvae in the small intestine. The larvae penetrate the cecal mucosa, travel to the lungs in blood or lymph, mature for 10 to 14 days, and are coughed up and swallowed. Ascaris suum is acquired from pig stool. Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they migrate to the lungs and are reswallowed, unlike Trichuris. Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris life cycle; larvae migrate through the lungs. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: migration of Ascaris larvae to alveoli causes Löffler syndrome with pulmonary eosinophilia (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they mig","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris "}},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old vegetarian woman who emigrated from rural Guatemala has new seizures. MRI shows multiple small cystic brain lesions. How did she most likely acquire the infection?","choices":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork","B":"Metacercariae on watercress","C":"Eggs from dog feces","D":"Cercariae in lake water","E":"Eggs from human feces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When humans ingest Taenia solium eggs in food or water contaminated with human feces, oncospheres penetrate the gut wall and spread hematogenously to form cysticerci, including in the brain. Adult tapeworms do not form by this route. Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tapeworm, not brain cysticerci, and she is vegetarian. Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticercosis. Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, whereas T. solium eggs come from human feces. Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocysticercosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brain cysts with seizures is the parasite hint for neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: viable cysticerci evade immunity with taeniaestatin and paramyosin (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tape","B":"Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticerc","C":"Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, wherea","D":"Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocyst"}},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker waded in a freshwater lake in East Africa. Months later she has hepatomegaly and eggs in her stool. Which mechanism allowed the parasite to enter her body?","choices":{"A":"Larval proteolytic enzymes","B":"Ingestion of snail tissue","C":"Bite of an infected black fly","D":"Inhalation of aerosolized eggs","E":"Ingestion of contaminated watercress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Schistosome cercariae swim in fresh water and penetrate human skin with proteolytic enzymes that degrade the keratinized layer, causing minimal skin reaction. S. mansoni affects mainly the liver and gut. Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, whereas schistosome cercariae penetrate skin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Schistosomiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension is the parasite hint for Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum (First Aid Microbiology (Trematodes); Parasite hints). COMLEX: females produce hundreds to thousands of eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"E":"Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, wh"}},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has nocturnal perianal itching that returns weeks after one course of treatment. He often sucks his thumb, and no one else at home is affected. What best explains his reinfection?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated drinking water","B":"Hand-to-mouth egg transfer","C":"Adult worm division in cecum","D":"Insect vector bite","E":"Eating undercooked meat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm females migrate out of the anus at night to deposit eggs on perianal skin. The eggs become infectious within about 6 hours and spread from the hands or bed linens, allowing self-reinfection. Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re-ingesting eggs. Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is spread fecal-orally and diagnosed with the tape test (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm is the most common helminthic infection in the United States (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re","E":"Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pi"}},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man from rural Kentucky takes high-dose prednisone after a lung transplant. He develops diffuse pulmonary infiltrates and Escherichia coli bacteremia, and larvae are found in his sputum. What is the source of the bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated central venous line","B":"Aspiration of gastric contents","C":"Larval damage to the bowel wall","D":"Infected urinary catheter","E":"Obstructed common bile duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Strongyloides hyperinfection, larvae invade the intestinal wall, and intestinal bacteria enter the blood through the damaged wall, causing sepsis. Worms of all stages may be found in the lungs and sputum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes pulmonary symptoms such as dry cough and hemoptysis (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hyperinfection larvae invade the colonic submucosa, lymphatics, and blood vessels (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from Jamaica with no history of immunosuppressive drug use develops overwhelming strongyloidiasis with larvae throughout his body. Which underlying infection most likely predisposed him?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C virus","B":"Latent Epstein-Barr virus","C":"High-risk papillomavirus","D":"Reactivated human cytomegalovirus","E":"Human T-lymphotropic virus 1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human T-lymphotropic virus type I infection predisposes to very high Strongyloides worm burdens through uncontrolled autoinfection, leading to fatal disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperinfection syndrome results from accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical South America, sub-Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl in rural Colombia passed a long roundworm last month. She now has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, and imaging shows a worm in the common bile duct. Which complication has developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute pancreatitis","B":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","C":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","D":"Splenic infarction","E":"Ureteral colic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ascaris worms that enter the biliary tree can cause acute pancreatitis and cholangitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may cause biliary obstruction and can emerge from the nose or mouth (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris infections are diagnosed by eggs in the stool or passed adult worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia who walks barefoot has fatigue and pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference 11.5–15.5 g/dL) and MCV is 68 fL (reference 77–95 fL). Stool shows nematode eggs. Which additional laboratory finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low serum folate","B":"Low serum ferritin","C":"High indirect bilirubin","D":"High methylmalonic acid","E":"Positive Coombs test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hookworms suck blood from the intestinal wall, and chronic blood loss causes iron deficiency with microcytic anemia and depleted iron stores. High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anemia comes from chronic blood loss. High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fish tapeworm, not the microcytic anemia of hookworm. A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookworm blood loss. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hookworm can also cause cutaneous larva migrans, a pruritic serpiginous rash (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae enter through skin after walking barefoot on contaminated beach or soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anem","D":"High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fi","E":"A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookw"}},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with nightly perianal itching develops vulvar itching and discharge. A tape test is positive. What mechanism explains her new symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Eggs carried in the bloodstream","B":"Larvae entering through the skin","C":"Bacteria spreading from the bladder","D":"Worms moving into the vagina","E":"Cysts forming in the vulvar tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterobius adults live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night. Worms moving into the vagina can cause vulvovaginitis. Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae entering through skin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is most common in children aged 5 to 10 years (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: very high pinworm burdens rarely cause rectal prolapse (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — appendicitis and anal canal lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae enterin"}},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old hunter ate undercooked bear meat 3 weeks ago. He has fever, myalgias, and periorbital edema, and the eosinophil fraction is 32% (reference range: 1–4%). Where are the parasites now located?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hepatic sinusoids","C":"Lumen of the cecum","D":"Intrahepatic bile ducts","E":"Renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trichinella larvae disseminate hematogenously and penetrate striated muscle cells, causing fever, myalgias, marked eosinophilia, and periorbital edema. The infected muscle cell gains a collagenous capsule and new blood vessels. Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 weeks the larvae have encysted in skeletal muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichinella larvae encyst in striated muscle after undercooked pork ingestion (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: humans are dead-end hosts for Trichinella (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 w"}},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has dry cough, wheezing, fever, and transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia. Two stool examinations show no eggs, but 2 months later he passes a large roundworm. Why were the early stool tests negative?","choices":{"A":"Eggs were destroyed by acid","B":"Larvae had not yet matured","C":"Worms were in the bile duct","D":"Infection was in the skin","E":"Eggs were shed in urine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The initial pulmonary stage of ascariasis causes Loeffler syndrome, a self-limited eosinophilic pneumonitis. Stool examination may not reveal the parasites if they have not yet matured in the intestine. Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin phase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löffler syndrome is pulmonary eosinophilia from Ascaris larvae in the alveoli (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: heavy Ascaris burdens can cause malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin pha"}},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old Ethiopian man who eats raw beef passes motile flat segments in his stool. The eosinophil fraction is 3% (reference range: 1–4%). What best explains the normal eosinophil count?","choices":{"A":"Steroid suppression of eosinophils","B":"Concurrent bacterial sepsis","C":"Adult worm confined to gut lumen","D":"Eosinophils trapped in spleen","E":"Low parasite egg output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In humans, Taenia saginata lives only in the gut lumen and does not form tissue cysticerci, so it lacks the tissue phase that drives strong Th2 eosinophilic responses. He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a luminal adult worm without tissue invasion. Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites are a classic cause of eosinophilia in the PACMAN mnemonic (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: tapeworm mature distal proglottids contain many eggs and detach into the feces (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a lumi","E":"Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does"}},{"id":"sdl35_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has 10 days of perianal itching that wakes her at night. A morning tape test is positive for pinworm eggs. Her CBC is normal, with hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which treatment plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mebendazole now and at 2 weeks","B":"Niclosamide as one single dose","C":"Oral ivermectin daily for 2 days","D":"Oral metronidazole for 7 days","E":"Oral vancomycin given for 10 days"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tape test eggs flattened on one side confirm Enterobius. Katzung gives mebendazole 100 mg once for pinworm, repeated at 2 weeks, because reinfection from newly hatched worms is common. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinworm, which is treated with mebendazole or albendazole. Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not pinworm. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm eggs are visualized via the tape test and treated with bendazoles or pyrantel pamoate (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm eggs mature rapidly and become infectious in about 6 hours (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm.","C":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinw","D":"Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba,","E":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths."}},{"id":"sdl35_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man from rural Kentucky has 3 months of intermittent diarrhea and bloating. His eosinophils are 14% (reference range: 1–3%). Stool microscopy shows motile larvae; no ova are seen. Which mechanism explains the action of the first-line drug for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Blocked tubulin polymerization in cells","B":"Intensified GABA signaling in nerves","C":"Increased calcium influx in tegument","D":"Blocked folate synthesis in cytoplasm","E":"Inhibited cell wall synthesis in worms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Larvae rather than ova in stool indicate Strongyloides stercoralis, whose larvae are mostly passed in the stool. Its drug of choice, ivermectin, paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission in peripheral nerves. Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechanism, not that of ivermectin, the Strongyloides drug of choice. Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action against flukes and tapeworms. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform larvae of Strongyloides are seen in feces under the microscope (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical regions and occurs sporadically in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechani","C":"Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action agai"}},{"id":"sdl35_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy who recently moved from rural Bangladesh passes a 20-cm pink worm from his rectum. Stool examination shows numerous eggs. His hemoglobin is 11.9 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which drug is first-line for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide","B":"Diethylcarbamazine","C":"Albendazole","D":"Doxycycline","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult worms 15 to 35 cm long passed from the rectum, with eggs in stool, diagnose Ascaris lumbricoides. Katzung lists albendazole, pyrantel pamoate, or mebendazole as the drugs of choice. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris. Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris has knobby-coated oval eggs in feces and is treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each adult female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day into the stool (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris.","B":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris.","E":"Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms."}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man in northern Minnesota has megaloblastic anemia, and his stool shows broad tapeworm segments. He eats home-cured raw lake fish. How did he acquire the parasite?","choices":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants","B":"Larvae in raw fish","C":"Bite of an infected sandfly","D":"Eggs shed by household dogs","E":"Cercariae in fresh lake water"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum, the fish tapeworm, is acquired by eating undercooked or raw fish and lives only in the gut. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the fish tapeworm competes for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the fish tapeworm does not form cysticerci in humans (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl with pinworm infection scratches her perianal skin at night. About how soon do freshly laid eggs become infectious?","choices":{"A":"About 6 hours","B":"About 6 days","C":"About 2 weeks","D":"About 6 weeks","E":"About 6 months"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enterobius eggs deposited on perianal skin mature rapidly and are infectious in approximately 6 hours, so scratching and hand-to-mouth spread allow quick reinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius causes anal pruritus that is worse at night (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: the eggs spread from hands or bed linens (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia has a heavy whipworm (Trichuris trichiura) burden with loose stools and anemia. Which complication is classically described in children?","choices":{"A":"Biliary obstruction","B":"Hepatic cysts","C":"Rectal prolapse","D":"Seizure disorder","E":"Myocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trichuris trichiura is often asymptomatic but can cause loose stools, anemia, and rectal prolapse in children. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover Trichuris; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichuris is spread fecal-orally and treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Trichuris infection is often asymptomatic, so symptoms track heavy worm burden (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":3,"stem":"A duodenal biopsy from a 38-year-old woman with mild strongyloidiasis shows larvae in the crypts without tissue invasion. Which inflammatory pattern is expected in the lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophil-rich infiltrate","B":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","C":"Noncaseating granulomas","D":"Dense plasma cell sheets","E":"Transmural lymphoid nodules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In mild strongyloidiasis, larvae are in the duodenal crypts but not the underlying tissue, with an eosinophil-rich lamina propria infiltrate and mucosal edema. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: in hyperinfection, many adult worms, larvae, and eggs fill the duodenal and ileal crypts with a mononuclear infiltrate (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman from eastern Tennessee has 4 months of intermittent loose stools and bloating. Her eosinophils are 11% (reference range: 1–3%). On a stool ova and parasite examination, which parasite form is most typical of the likely nematode?","choices":{"A":"Plugged barrel eggs","B":"Rhabditoid larvae","C":"Operculated eggs","D":"Mammillated eggs","E":"Gravid proglottids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Strongyloides stercoralis produces eggs in the intestinal mucosa, but most larvae hatch and are passed in the stool, so stool examination shows rhabditoid larvae. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes duodenitis, dry cough, and pruritus, and hyperinfection occurs with immunosuppression (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides females reproduce by parthenogenesis in the small-intestinal mucosa (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had cystic brain lesions without symptoms for years. He now has new seizures, and imaging shows edema surrounding one lesion. What best explains the change?","choices":{"A":"Adult worm growth in the brain","B":"Cyst rupture into the bloodstream","C":"New egg release within the cyst","D":"Inflammation around a dying cyst","E":"Bacterial abscess within the cyst"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Viable Taenia solium cysts often do not cause symptoms and evade immunity, but an inflammatory response develops when cysticerci die and degenerate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: larval cysts in tissues outside the intestine do not develop into mature worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true}],"36":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Benzimidazoles such as mebendazole are thought to kill nematodes by inhibiting what?","choices":{"A":"Ergosterol synthesis","B":"Folate synthesis","C":"Peptidoglycan synthesis","D":"Chitin synthesis","E":"Microtubule synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Benzimidazoles, including mebendazole and albendazole, are thought to act against nematodes by inhibiting microtubule synthesis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mebendazole binds tubulin and is listed with the microtubule-acting drugs (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: less than 10% of an oral mebendazole dose is absorbed (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibiotic kills adult filarial worms indirectly by eliminating their Wolbachia bacterial symbionts?","choices":{"A":"Doxycycline","B":"Azithromycin","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Vancomycin","E":"Clindamycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Doxycycline has macrofilaricidal activity against Wuchereria bancrofti and activity against onchocerciasis because it kills Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial symbiont of filarial parasites. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Doxycycline). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration and are generally avoided in pregnancy and in children, except doxycycline (First Aid Antimicrobials — Tetracyclines). COMLEX: Katzung reports better activity against adult W bancrofti than any other available drug (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes by increasing their membrane permeability to which ion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride","B":"Sodium","C":"Calcium","D":"Potassium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, causing paralysis, dislodgement, and death of the worm. Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated channels is ivermectin's mechanism, not praziquantel's. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia infections (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel tablets are swallowed without chewing because the bitter taste can induce retching (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated cha"}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice is used for lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti and for loiasis?","choices":{"A":"Diethylcarbamazine","B":"Triclabendazole","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Piperazine citrate","E":"Niclosamide tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diethylcarbamazine is a drug of choice for W bancrofti, Brugia, and Loa loa because of its efficacy and lack of serious toxicity; it immobilizes microfilariae and exposes them to host defenses. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica, not filariasis. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris and pinworm, not filariae. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa (deer fly) and Wuchereria bancrofti (mosquito) are both treated with diethylcarbamazine (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: the plasma half-life of diethylcarbamazine is longer in alkaline urine (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica,","C":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris an"}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For neurocysticercosis, why is albendazole now generally preferred over praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Avoids need for corticosteroids","B":"Single oral dose regimen","C":"No liver toxicity with long use","D":"Better subarachnoid penetration","E":"Proven safety in early pregnancy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albendazole is preferred for neurocysticercosis because of a shorter course, lower cost, improved penetration into the subarachnoid space, and higher drug levels with corticosteroids. Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actually rise when steroids are co-administered. Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-dose regimen. Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires liver monitoring. Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole, while intestinal taeniasis is treated with praziquantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: corticosteroids are usually given with the antihelminthic to limit inflammation from dying organisms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actua","B":"Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-","C":"Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires ","E":"Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy."}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For uncomplicated strongyloidiasis, which drug is given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Niclosamide","D":"Oxamniquine","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis, given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days; thiabendazole is an alternative but is more toxic. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm, whereas ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is treated with ivermectin or bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: in disseminated disease in immunosuppressed patients, monthly suppressive therapy may help (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides.","E":"Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm, whereas ivermec"}},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old sheep farmer with a large hepatic hydatid cyst starts several months of albendazole before surgery. Which monitoring is advised during this long course?","choices":{"A":"Blood counts and liver tests","B":"Serum creatinine and urinalysis","C":"Serial ECG for QT changes","D":"Audiometry and visual fields","E":"Serum magnesium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-term albendazole for hydatid disease is usually well tolerated but can cause raised liver enzymes and pancytopenia, so blood counts and liver function are monitored. Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not the kidneys, so creatinine and urinalysis are not the advised monitoring. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Echinococcus hydatid cysts are treated with albendazole, with surgery for some cysts (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole should be avoided in patients with cirrhosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not th"}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks' gestation has perianal itching, and a tape test shows pinworm eggs. Her physician defers mebendazole. What property of mebendazole explains this?","choices":{"A":"Causes neonatal gray syndrome","B":"Inhibits fetal folate uptake","C":"Causes fetal tooth staining","D":"Teratogenic in animal studies","E":"Causes fetal renal tubule damage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mebendazole is teratogenic in animals and is therefore contraindicated in pregnancy. Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern, not a mebendazole one. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: for pinworm, mebendazole 100 mg is given once and repeated at 2 weeks (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern"}},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from Ghana with onchocerciasis receives a single oral dose of the drug of choice, a macrocyclic lactone. Concurrent use of which drug class should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Beta-blockers","D":"ACE inhibitors","E":"Benzodiazepines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission, so it is best not combined with other GABA-enhancing drugs such as barbiturates, benzodiazepines, or valproic acid. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus causes black skin nodules and river blindness (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: treatment reactions in onchocerciasis come mainly from killing microfilariae and peak on day 2 (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Cameroon has a worm crossing his conjunctiva and a very high Loa loa microfilarial load, well above the 2500/mL high-risk level. Which initial approach is safest?","choices":{"A":"Immediate full-dose diethylcarbamazine","B":"Single-dose praziquantel","C":"Slower-acting albendazole first","D":"Topical thiabendazole cream","E":"Weekly oral niclosamide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For heavy Loa loa burdens (more than 2500 parasites/mL), Katzung lists apheresis or therapy with albendazole, which is slower acting and better tolerated, before definitive filaricidal treatment. Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy and retinal hemorrhage, so burden is lowered first. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in loiasis. Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and larva migrans, not loiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate; Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa causes skin swelling and a worm in the conjunctiva (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: reactions to dying microfilariae are occasionally severe in L loa infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy an","B":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in ","D":"Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and lar"}},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with symptomatic parenchymal neurocysticercosis is taking dexamethasone for cerebral edema. What effect does dexamethasone have on praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Reduced plasma drug levels","B":"Increased plasma drug levels","C":"Prolonged elimination half-life","D":"Greater CSF drug penetration","E":"Faster cyst calcification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Corticosteroids can decrease plasma praziquantel levels by up to 50%, whereas albendazole levels increase, one reason albendazole is preferred when steroids are needed. Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is albendazole levels that rise. Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an effect of dexamethasone on praziquantel. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium cysticercosis follows ingestion of eggs in fecally contaminated food (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: some workers caution against praziquantel in spinal neurocysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is","D":"Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an"}},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man from Peru has right upper quadrant pain, eosinophilia, and Fasciola hepatica eggs after eating watercress. Why is praziquantel a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Excessive toxicity in hepatic disease","B":"Lack of activity in this infection","C":"Poor oral bioavailability in adults","D":"Need for prolonged IV dosing","E":"High rates of liver fluke relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Praziquantel treats most trematodes but was not effective for fascioliasis even at high doses; triclabendazole is the drug of choice, with bithionol as an alternative. Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem in fascioliasis is Fasciola resistance. Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis because Fasciola is resistant. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Triclabendazole; Bithionol). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats trematodes such as Schistosoma and Clonorchis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: triclabendazole may prolong the QT interval (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem","D":"Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis becau"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with hydatid liver disease starts albendazole. How should he take it for this tissue infection?","choices":{"A":"With a fatty meal","B":"On an empty stomach","C":"With an antacid dose","D":"With grapefruit juice","E":"At bedtime with milk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Albendazole is taken on an empty stomach for intraluminal parasites but with a fatty meal for tissue parasites, which increases its erratic absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: albendazole is used for Echinococcus and neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole is converted on first pass to the active metabolite albendazole sulfoxide (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has strongyloidiasis. Why has thiabendazole been largely replaced by other agents?","choices":{"A":"Lower cure rates than other agents","B":"Greater toxicity than other agents","C":"Need for prolonged IV infusions","D":"Widespread parasite drug resistance","E":"Poor absorption from the gut"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiabendazole is much more toxic than other benzimidazoles and ivermectin; irreversible liver failure and fatal Stevens-Johnson syndrome have been reported. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bendazoles treat most intestinal nematodes (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: thiabendazole cream can be applied topically for cutaneous larva migrans (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — worm burden and autoinfection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with river blindness receives a single 8-mg oral dose of a drug approved in 2018 with a half-life of 3–4 weeks. Which drug is it?","choices":{"A":"Triclabendazole","B":"Oxamniquine","C":"Moxidectin","D":"Niclosamide","E":"Metrifonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Moxidectin, a macrocyclic lactone approved in 2018 for onchocerciasis in persons 12 years or older, is given once orally and has a half-life of 3–4 weeks. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Moxidectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus is spread by the female black fly (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: moxidectin lowered skin microfilarial loads more than the older standard drug in a recent trial (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with fascioliasis has a long QT interval, so the drug of choice is withheld. Which alternative is listed for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Bithionol","B":"Praziquantel","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Mebendazole","E":"Oxamniquine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bithionol is an alternative to triclabendazole for fascioliasis and to praziquantel for paragonimiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Bithionol; Triclabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: liver flukes such as Clonorchis are acquired from undercooked fish (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: skin rashes after a week of bithionol suggest a reaction to antigens from dying worms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy with hookworm anemia is given albendazole rather than mebendazole. What is the main reason?","choices":{"A":"Lower cost per complete course of care","B":"Safety in the first trimester","C":"Once-weekly dosing schedule","D":"Better efficacy in this infection","E":"Topical form for skin lesions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For hookworm infection and trichuriasis, albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days is recommended, with improved efficacy over mebendazole for hookworm. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ancylostoma and Necator cause microcytic anemia and are treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: mebendazole cure rates for hookworm are low, though worm burden falls sharply (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has many viable parenchymal brain cysts from Taenia solium. Which regimen improves the parasiticidal effect?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide plus mebendazole","B":"Pyrantel plus piperazine","C":"Thiabendazole plus pyrantel","D":"Metrifonate plus oxamniquine","E":"Albendazole plus praziquantel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Combining albendazole with praziquantel improves efficacy in patients with multiple brain cysts. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cysticercosis is treated with albendazole, plus praziquantel in some cases (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel is contraindicated in ocular cysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"}],"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Metoclopramide speeds gastric emptying and raises lower esophageal sphincter pressure mainly by what action?","choices":{"A":"Muscarinic M3 receptor agonism","B":"Dopamine D2 receptor blockade","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor blockade","D":"Histamine H2 receptor blockade","E":"Mu opioid receptor agonism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist; blocking inhibitory dopamine receptors in the gut raises LES pressure, enhances esophageal peristalsis and speeds gastric emptying, with little effect on small bowel or colon. Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not metoclopramide's. 5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoclopramide's prokinetic effect. Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather than speeding gastric emptying. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist used for diabetic gastroparesis and persistent GERD (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide also blocks D2 receptors in the area postrema chemoreceptor trigger zone (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not me","C":"5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoc","E":"Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather "}},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent given intravenously is used to treat acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)?","choices":{"A":"Atropine","B":"Octreotide","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Neostigmine","E":"Dicyclomine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, raises acetylcholine at enteric synapses; a 2 mg IV dose rapidly evacuates colonic gas and stool in acute pseudo-obstruction. Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would worsen colonic pseudo-obstruction. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neostigmine is a quaternary (charged) AChE inhibitor that does not enter the CNS and treats postoperative ileus and urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: cholinergic effects of the IV dose include salivation, vomiting and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would w"}},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does domperidone cause extrapyramidal effects far less often than metoclopramide?","choices":{"A":"Poor blood-brain barrier entry","B":"Faster renal clearance of the drug","C":"Weaker binding at gut D2 receptors","D":"Added serotonin 5-HT4 agonism","E":"Greater pituitary prolactin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Domperidone shares the D2-blocking mechanism but crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so central extrapyramidal effects are rare. Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic mechanism as metoclopramide, so weaker gut binding is not the reason. Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CNS sparing reflects poor blood-brain barrier entry. Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not explain fewer extrapyramidal effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: centrally acting D2 blockers cause parkinsonism and acute dystonia as drug-induced movement disorders (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone still acts at the area postrema, which lies outside the barrier (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic m","D":"Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CN","E":"Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not "}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Alosetron use for diarrhea-predominant IBS is restricted mainly because of what serious adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Torsades de pointes","B":"Ischemic colitis","C":"Agranulocytosis","D":"QT shortening","E":"Hepatic necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alosetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist, causes constipation in up to 30% and ischemic colitis in up to 3 per 1000 patients, so it is restricted to women with severe IBS with diarrhea. Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-HT4 agonists like cisapride, not alosetron. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are antiemetics whose adverse effects include headache and constipation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on extrinsic afferents that carry visceral pain signals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-"}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Lubiprostone relieves constipation-predominant IBS by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2 chloride channel activation","B":"Mu opioid receptor activation in gut","C":"Na+/K+ ATPase pump inhibition","D":"Bulk retention of stool water","E":"5-HT3 receptor inhibition on afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lubiprostone, a prostanoic acid derivative, activates type 2 chloride channels (ClC-2) on the apical enterocyte membrane, increasing chloride-rich fluid secretion into the lumen. Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and treats diarrhea, not constipation. Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psyllium, not lubiprostone. 5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and causes constipation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Chloride Channel Activator). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lubiprostone appears in the anticonstipation table with diarrhea and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: lubiprostone is approved for women with IBS-C and avoided in women who may become pregnant (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and ","D":"Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psylli","E":"5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and caus"}},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Eluxadoline for diarrhea-predominant IBS should be avoided in a patient with which history?","choices":{"A":"Prior appendectomy","B":"Hypothyroidism","C":"Prior pancreatitis","D":"Recurrent gout attacks","E":"Migraine headaches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eluxadoline, a mu-opioid agonist, can cause sphincter of Oddi spasm with pancreatitis, so it is avoided with prior pancreatitis, alcoholism or sphincter of Oddi disease. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripherally acting mu agonists slow gut motility with low addiction potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a lower 75 mg dose is used after cholecystectomy because sphincter of Oddi complications are more common (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide is an analog of which gut hormone?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon-like peptide 1","B":"Glucose insulinotropic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Glucagon-like peptide 2","E":"Pancreatic polypeptide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog used for short-bowel syndrome; GLP-2 is an intestinotrophic peptide released from L cells. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs such as exenatide are incretin drugs, unlike the intestinotrophic analog used for short gut (First Aid Endocrine Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide binds receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"By which route is teduglutide given for short-bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Weekly intravenous infusion pump","B":"Twice-daily oral tablet","C":"Monthly intramuscular depot","D":"Daily rectal suppository","E":"Daily subcutaneous injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teduglutide is given at 0.05 mg/kg once daily by subcutaneous injection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog given by subcutaneous injection (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: teduglutide dosing is weight based at 0.05 mg/kg per day (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide helps patients with short-bowel syndrome mainly by promoting what?","choices":{"A":"Faster emptying of the stomach","B":"Slower colonic transit","C":"Mucosal epithelial growth","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output","E":"Hepatic bile acid output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Through trophic hormones, teduglutide stimulates mucosal epithelial growth of the remaining bowel and enhances fluid absorption. Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than speeding it. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: short-bowel syndrome causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluid after extensive resection (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide acts on enteric neurons and endocrine cells to adapt the residual gut (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than spe"}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetic gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for 2 years. She now has repetitive lip smacking and tongue thrusting that persist after the drug is stopped. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonic reaction","B":"Akathisia restlessness","C":"Serotonin syndrome","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Essential tremor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-term metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia that may be irreversible, so prolonged use is avoided, especially in older adults. Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy, whereas persistent movements after long-term use indicate tardive dyskinesia. Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent orofacial movements of tardive dyskinesia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed orofacial choreiform movements follow long-term D2 blockade by antipsychotics or prokinetics (First Aid Pharmacology (drug adverse effects)). COMLEX: tardive movements are more likely with long-term use in older patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy, whereas persistent m","B":"Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent oro"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman takes metoclopramide for postsurgical gastroparesis. Months later she has milky nipple discharge and missed periods. Which laboratory finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum prolactin","B":"High serum cortisol","C":"High serum prolactin","D":"Low free thyroxine level","E":"High serum calcium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin release; D2 blockade raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea, amenorrhea and gynecomastia. Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galactorrhea, not lowering it. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine antagonists are a drug cause of hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone is used off-label for postpartum lactation through the same pituitary effect (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galac"}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with diabetic gastroparesis improves on oral erythromycin, which induces fasting migrating motor complexes. After several weeks the benefit fades despite the same dose. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid breakdown of the drug","B":"Rapid development of tolerance","C":"Induced CYP3A4 metabolism","D":"Selection of resistant flora","E":"Worsening autonomic damage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and initiates the migrating motor complex, which normally recurs about every 90 minutes during fasting; tolerance develops rapidly, limiting long-term use. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Macrolides). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 and cause QT prolongation as notable interactions (First Aid Antimicrobials (macrolides)). COMLEX: a single IV macrolide dose of 3 mg/kg can clear the stomach of blood before upper endoscopy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — migrating motor complex during fasting","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has IBS with diarrhea: frequent loose stools and urgency but mild pain. She wants an inexpensive over-the-counter medicine. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Psyllium","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Neomycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loperamide, a peripherally acting mu-opioid agonist, reduces stool frequency and urgency in IBS with diarrhea; it does not relieve abdominal pain. Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator for IBS-C and would worsen diarrhea. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: antidiarrheal mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and cause constipation and nausea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: diphenoxylate is combined with atropine to discourage overdosage (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator fo"}},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has IBS with constipation and bloating. Fiber and over-the-counter laxatives have not helped. He wants a capsule taken each morning that raises enterocyte cGMP. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Lubiprostone","B":"Linaclotide","C":"Alosetron","D":"Eluxadoline","E":"Hyoscyamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Linaclotide (290 mcg daily) activates guanylyl cyclase C, raising cGMP, which opens CFTR and increases chloride and bicarbonate secretion; lubiprostone is approved in women. Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would worsen constipation. Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits diarrhea, not constipation. Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does not raise cGMP. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Guanylate Cyclase C Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GC-C agonists such as plecanatide cause diarrhea, bloating and flatulence (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GC-C agonists are contraindicated in children because of dehydration risk (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would","D":"Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits di","E":"Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does "}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has mixed-type IBS, alternating stool form with chronic abdominal pain as her main complaint. Which nightly drug best targets the pain?","choices":{"A":"High-dose bismuth salts","B":"Daily lactulose syrup by mouth","C":"Oral metoclopramide","D":"Low-dose amitriptyline","E":"Weekly erythromycin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants alter central processing of visceral afferent input and help chronic IBS pain; their anticholinergic effect also slows stools. Katzung does not separate mixed-type therapy, so this item is objectives-first. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tricyclic antidepressants cause anticholinergic, sedative and cardiac conduction effects (First Aid Psychiatry Pharmacology). COMLEX: desipramine is dosed at 10 to 50 mg per day for chronic IBS pain (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had massive small-bowel resection after a volvulus and needs home intravenous nutrition 6 nights a week. Teduglutide is started. Which outcome best shows that it is working?","choices":{"A":"Higher fasting glucose","B":"Faster gastric emptying","C":"Lower serum prolactin level","D":"Rise in serum bilirubin","E":"Reduced infusion frequency"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In trials, 54% of patients given teduglutide cut parenteral support by at least 1 day per week, versus 23% given placebo. Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral support is the measure of benefit. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: extensive resection removes absorptive surface and causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluids (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: patients with under 200 cm of small intestine usually need partial or complete parenteral support (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral "}},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with short-bowel syndrome is being considered for teduglutide. Which screening is most important because of a known drug risk?","choices":{"A":"Echocardiography for valves","B":"Bone density scanning","C":"Colonoscopy for neoplasia","D":"Audiometry for hearing","E":"Slit-lamp eye examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teduglutide is trophic to the mucosa and may raise the risk of colorectal and small-bowel neoplasia, so colonoscopy is done before and during therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trophic stimulation of intestinal epithelium raises concern for adenomatous growth (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: GLP-2 analog therapy carries a neoplasia warning for colon and small bowel (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome after Crohn resections starts teduglutide. Its effect on the remaining mucosa is mediated downstream by release of which hormone?","choices":{"A":"Insulin-like growth factor","B":"Histamine from mast cells","C":"Somatostatin from D cells","D":"Substance P from neurons","E":"Gastrin from antral G cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide binds enteric neurons and endocrine cells and stimulates release of trophic hormones, including insulin-like growth factor, that drive epithelial growth and fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP from enteric neurons increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Physiology (GI regulatory substances)). COMLEX: octreotide may be used for high-output diarrhea in short-bowel syndrome (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has urinary retention and slow gut transit. Why is bethanechol seldom used for GI motility today?","choices":{"A":"Rapid loss of the drug's effect","B":"Frequent tardive movement disorders","C":"Widespread cholinergic actions","D":"Serious hepatic toxicity","E":"Marked drop in white blood cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bethanechol stimulates M3 receptors on muscle and myenteric neurons but is seldom used for GI motility because of its broad cholinergic effects and newer alternatives. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -chol suffix marks a direct muscarinic agonist resistant to AChE that treats urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: bethanechol still sees occasional use for postoperative or neurogenic bladder retention (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with chronic constipation and long QT starts prucalopride. Why is it safer than older 5-HT4 agonists?","choices":{"A":"Little CYP3A4 hepatic metabolism","B":"Little hERG channel affinity","C":"Strong D2 receptor blockade","D":"Shorter plasma half-life","E":"Partial muscarinic blockade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cisapride and tegaserod were withdrawn for cardiovascular events; prucalopride is a high-affinity 5-HT4 agonist without significant hERG K+ channel or 5-HT1B affinity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Serotonin 5-HT4-Receptor Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT4 agonism stimulates enteric nerves to increase peristalsis and secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: prucalopride dosing falls to 1 mg daily when creatinine clearance is below 30 mL/min (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with IBS with constipation bloats badly on psyllium. Which inexpensive agent is a sensible next step?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate tablets","B":"Alosetron tablets","C":"Cholestyramine","D":"Polyethylene glycol","E":"Bismuth subsalicylate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fiber can worsen bloating in IBS with constipation; osmotic laxatives such as polyethylene glycol or milk of magnesia soften stools and are often tried next. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives draw water into the lumen and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: bulk-forming fiber can increase gas and distension in IBS (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with IBS cramping takes dicyclomine. Which adverse effect is most likely at higher doses?","choices":{"A":"Slow heart rate","B":"Excess salivation","C":"Blurred near vision","D":"Loose watery stools","E":"Pinpoint pupils"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dicyclomine and hyoscyamine are antimuscarinic antispasmodics that give short-term cramp relief; higher doses cause dry mouth, blurred near vision and constipation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: muscarinic antagonists cause dry mouth, mydriasis and cycloplegia (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: antispasmodic benefit in IBS is short term and modest (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman had extensive small-bowel resection. Below what residual length do such patients usually need IV nutrition?","choices":{"A":"Less than 20 cm","B":"Less than 600 cm","C":"Less than 400 cm","D":"Less than 200 cm","E":"Less than 50 cm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with less than 200 cm of small intestine, with or without colon resection, usually depend on partial or complete parenteral support. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of absorptive length impairs absorption of fluids and nutrients (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the adult small intestine is several meters long, so short-bowel syndrome reflects major loss (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome starts teduglutide. Which additional effect of the drug reduces his stool output?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced fluid absorption","B":"Faster colonic transit","C":"Reduced hepatic bile flow","D":"Increased mucus output","E":"Greater gut colonization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide stimulates trophic hormone release that promotes epithelial growth and enhances intestinal fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volume of fluid lost after resection reflects reduced absorptive surface (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GLP-2 receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells mediate the trophic response (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Docusate softens stool mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Lowering surface tension","B":"Drawing water in by osmosis","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel","E":"Opening apical ion channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Docusate is a stool surfactant that lets water and lipids penetrate the stool; it is often given to hospitalized patients to minimize straining. Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxatives such as PEG and lactulose, not docusate. Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not docusate. Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as psyllium, not the surfactant docusate. Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as lubiprostone and linaclotide, not docusate. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emollient laxatives are surfactants that promote water entry into stool (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: glycerin suppositories are another stool surfactant option given rectally (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxativ","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxat","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as p","E":"Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as "}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"A woman has used an anthraquinone laxative daily for years. Which colonoscopy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestone mucosa","C":"Melanosis coli","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aloe, senna and cascara are hydrolyzed in the colon; chronic use causes brown colonic pigmentation called melanosis coli, without a proven link to colorectal cancer. Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic anthraquinone use. Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxative use. Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative colitis, not anthraquinone pigmentation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anthraquinone Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives act by enteric nerve stimulation and colonic contraction, with diarrhea as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: oral anthraquinones act in 6 to 12 hours and rectal doses within 2 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic a","B":"Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxati","D":"Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative c"}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term use of mineral oil as a lubricant laxative can cause which nutritional problem?","choices":{"A":"Copper deficiency anemia","B":"Iron overload in the liver","C":"Zinc deficiency rash","D":"Fat-soluble vitamin loss","E":"Excess vitamin C levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mineral oil coats stool and retards water absorption; long-term use impairs absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, and aspiration can cause lipid pneumonitis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K depends on bile emulsification, and mineral oil intake can cause their deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, fat-soluble vitamins). COMLEX: mineral oil is used for fecal impaction in young children and debilitated adults (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do diphenoxylate tablets contain a small amount of atropine?","choices":{"A":"Enhancing pain relief","B":"Preventing nausea","C":"Discouraging abuse","D":"Delaying tolerance","E":"Speeding absorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diphenoxylate is a prescription opioid agonist with CNS effects and dependence at higher doses; 0.025 mg atropine is added to discourage overdosage and may add to the antidiarrheal action. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Opioid Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diphenoxylate is a mu agonist that slows gut motility with constipation and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: opioids slow colonic transit by inhibiting presynaptic cholinergic nerves in the enteric plexuses (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term somatostatin-analog therapy for acromegaly most commonly causes which problem?","choices":{"A":"Kidney calcium oxalate stones","B":"Severe symptomatic hypercalcemia","C":"Sustained sinus tachycardia","D":"Hyperthyroid storm crisis","E":"Gallbladder sludge or stones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting gallbladder contractility and altering fat absorption, long-term octreotide causes sludge or gallstones in over 50% of patients; it can also cause steatorrhea, hyperglycemia, hypothyroidism and bradycardia. Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia. Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid storm. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs raise cholelithiasis risk through CCK inhibition (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a monthly intramuscular depot form of the somatostatin analog is available (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia.","D":"Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid st"}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Colestipol for chronic diarrhea should be taken at least 2 hours apart from other medicines. Why?","choices":{"A":"It induces hepatic CYP3A4","B":"It binds many oral drugs","C":"It raises gastric pH","D":"It blocks P-glycoprotein","E":"It speeds gastric emptying"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholestyramine and colestipol bind many drugs and reduce their absorption, so they are separated from other drugs by 2 hours; colesevelam has little effect on drug absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins cause GI upset and decreased absorption of other drugs (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin adverse effects include bloating, flatulence, constipation and fecal impaction (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Sulfasalazine releases active 5-aminosalicylic acid in the colon through which process?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial azoreductase cleavage","B":"Hepatic CYP450 oxidation of it","C":"Gastric acid hydrolysis of the bond","D":"Pancreatic esterase cleavage","E":"pH-dependent resin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sulfasalazine links 5-ASA to sulfapyridine by an azo bond; colonic bacteria cleave it with azoreductase, releasing 5-ASA in the terminal ileum and colon. pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, whereas sulfasalazine is an azo compound cleaved by colonic bacteria. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine combines antibacterial sulfapyridine with anti-inflammatory 5-ASA and is activated by colonic bacteria (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: mesalamine products instead use timed-release granules or pH-sensitive coatings (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"E":"pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, w"}},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient starting sulfasalazine for ulcerative colitis should take which daily supplement?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Vitamin D3","C":"Iron sulfate","D":"Folic acid","E":"Zinc salts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so 1 mg/d folic acid is recommended. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine adverse effects include malaise, nausea, sulfonamide toxicity and reversible oligospermia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: slow acetylators of sulfapyridine have more frequent and severe adverse effects (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Before starting azathioprine for Crohn disease, which test helps predict severe marrow toxicity?","choices":{"A":"G6PD enzyme activity","B":"TPMT enzyme activity","C":"HLA-B*5701 typing","D":"Serum ferritin level","E":"CYP2C19 genotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiopurine methyltransferase catabolizes 6-MP; activity is low in 11% and absent in 0.3% of people, raising active thioguanine nucleotides and marrow suppression risk. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine is an antimetabolite precursor of 6-mercaptopurine that causes pancytopenia (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: complete blood counts and liver tests are monitored in every patient on purine analogs (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has taken milk of magnesia nightly for months. He now has weakness, absent reflexes and bradycardia. Which disturbance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypokalemia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hyperphosphatemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Magnesium hydroxide should not be used for long periods in renal insufficiency because of the risk of hypermagnesemia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium-containing laxatives provide an osmotic load and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the colon neither concentrates nor dilutes fecal fluid, so nonabsorbed solute obligates stool water (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman with mild renal insufficiency takes sodium phosphate tablets for colonoscopy preparation. A week later her creatinine has doubled. Biopsy would most likely show which change?","choices":{"A":"Urate nephropathy","B":"Nephrocalcinosis","C":"Oxalate nephropathy","D":"Myoglobin casts","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium phosphate can cause hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia, and acute renal failure from tubular calcium phosphate deposition (nephrocalcinosis); it is avoided in frail, elderly or renal patients. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic agents draw water into the lumen and can be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: balanced isotonic lavage solutions avoid intravascular fluid and electrolyte shifts during preparation (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old bed-bound nursing home resident has had no bowel movement for 4 days. Staff want a stimulant laxative that works within an hour. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral senna tablets","B":"Oral psyllium powder","C":"Oral docusate capsule","D":"Bisacodyl suppository","E":"Oral lactulose syrup"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bisacodyl, a diphenylmethane stimulant, works in 30 to 60 minutes rectally versus 6 to 10 hours orally, with minimal systemic absorption. Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an hour like rectal bisacodyl. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts slowly. Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not a fast-acting stimulant. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does not act within an hour. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Diphenylmethane Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives cause colonic contraction through enteric nerve stimulation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: long-term stimulant use is probably safe and may be needed in neurologically impaired patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an","B":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts ","C":"Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not ","E":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does "}},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease. He now has watery postprandial diarrhea without steatorrhea. Which drug best treats it?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Pancrelipase","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Diphenoxylate","E":"Metronidazole tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal disease or resection causes malabsorbed bile salts that trigger colonic secretory diarrhea; bile salt-binding resins reduce it, though they can worsen fat malabsorption if the bile pool is depleted. Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bind the malabsorbed bile acids driving this secretory diarrhea. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins prevent intestinal reabsorption of bile acids, prompting the liver to use cholesterol to make more (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin powders are taken one to three times daily before meals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"D":"Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bin"}},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with metastatic carcinoid tumor has flushing, wheezing and voluminous watery diarrhea. Her liver lesions cannot be removed surgically. Which drug best controls her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate","B":"Octreotide","C":"Bismuth","D":"Lubiprostone","E":"Prednisone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide inhibits hormone secretion by carcinoid and VIPoma tumors, reducing secretory diarrhea and flushing, and may slow tumor progression. Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormone secretion or control carcinoid flushing. Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and would worsen diarrhea. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome is diagnosed by urinary 5-HIAA and treated with a somatostatin analog (First Aid Endocrine, carcinoid syndrome). COMLEX: higher subcutaneous doses of 100 to 250 mcg inhibit motility in dumping or vagotomy diarrhea (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormo","D":"Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and wo"}},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, a temperature of 39.4°C and abdominal tenderness after eating undercooked chicken. Why should loperamide be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Risk of central opioid dependence","B":"Risk of respiratory depression in adults","C":"Risk of severe serotonin syndrome","D":"Risk of worsened underlying illness","E":"Risk of tardive movement disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antidiarrheals are safe for mild to moderate acute diarrhea but should not be used with bloody diarrhea, high fever or systemic toxicity because they may worsen the underlying condition. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so central dependence is not the concern. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respiratory depression is not the concern. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and low addictive potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: antidiarrheals are stopped when diarrhea worsens despite therapy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so centra","B":"Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respir"}},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Crohn disease is maintained on azathioprine. She is started on allopurinol for gout and 3 weeks later has a white cell count of 1,100/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). What explains this?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal drug excretion","B":"Increased drug absorption","C":"Reduced 6-MP breakdown","D":"Allopurinol marrow allergy","E":"Folate antagonism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase catabolism of the purine analogs, increasing active 6-thioguanine nucleotides and causing severe leukopenia. Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase breakdown of azathioprine. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6-MP is degraded by xanthine oxidase, so its toxicity rises with allopurinol (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: febuxostat raises toxicity the same way, and purine-analog benefit in IBD has a median delay of 17 weeks because thioguanine nucleotides accumulate slowly (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopu"}},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with fistulizing Crohn disease who emigrated from the Philippines will begin infliximab. Which test must be done first?","choices":{"A":"Latent tuberculosis test","B":"Serum ceruloplasmin level","C":"Hepatitis A antibody titer","D":"Stool culture panel","E":"Repeat colonic biopsy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti-TNF therapy suppresses the TH1 response; all patients need a tuberculin skin test or interferon gamma release assay first, and positive patients get prophylaxis before therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF is important in granuloma formation and stabilization, and anti-TNF drugs can cause drug-induced lupus (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: serious infection risk rises markedly with concurrent corticosteroids (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has mild to moderate Crohn disease of the ileum and ascending colon. She wants a glucocorticoid with fewer systemic adverse effects. Which is best?","choices":{"A":"Oral prednisone at high dose","B":"Hydrocortisone retention enema","C":"IV methylprednisolone","D":"Oral dexamethasone daily","E":"Controlled-release budesonide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budesonide has high glucocorticoid receptor affinity but extensive CYP3A4 first-pass metabolism; the distal ileum and colon release form treats mild to moderate ileocolonic Crohn disease with less systemic toxicity. High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the systemic adverse effects she wants to avoid. Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot reach the ileum and ascending colon. IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease, not a low-systemic option for mild disease. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inactivate NF-kB and inhibit synthesis of almost all cytokines (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: potent CYP3A4 inhibitors raise plasma levels of the controlled-release steroid severalfold (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the system","B":"Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot","C":"IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman on lactulose for constipation has cramps and flatus. What causes these effects?","choices":{"A":"Colonic bacterial fermentation","B":"Small-bowel enzyme deficiency","C":"Direct irritation of the mucosa","D":"Enteric nerve stimulation","E":"Gastric acid hypersecretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sorbitol and lactulose are nonabsorbable sugars metabolized by colonic bacteria, producing flatus and cramps; PEG does not. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy by trapping NH4+ in the colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: high-dose osmotic purgatives evacuate the bowel within 1 to 3 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man starts psyllium for mild constipation. How does it act?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant softening of stool","B":"Direct enteric nerve activation","C":"Mu opioid receptor blockade","D":"Water-absorbing bulky gel","E":"Apical chloride channel opening"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bulk-forming laxatives are indigestible hydrophilic colloids that absorb water to form a bulky gel, distending the colon and promoting peristalsis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bulk-Forming Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: soluble fibers draw water into the lumen and form a viscous liquid, with bloating as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: polycarbophil is a synthetic bulk-forming fiber alongside psyllium and methylcellulose (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman on several heart medicines needs a bile acid resin for diarrhea. Which choice has the fewest drug-binding interactions?","choices":{"A":"Colestipol","B":"Sucralfate","C":"Kaolin-pectin","D":"Activated charcoal","E":"Colesevelam"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colesevelam does not appear to affect absorption of other drugs significantly, unlike older resins that must be separated from other medicines. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins lower LDL by interrupting enterohepatic bile acid recycling (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: removing more bile acids from a small pool can worsen fat malabsorption (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has scleroderma with intestinal pseudo-obstruction. A somatostatin analog is given at 50 mcg SC. What is its intended effect?","choices":{"A":"Decreased gastric acid output","B":"Increased pancreatic secretion","C":"Increased small-bowel motility","D":"Decreased portal venous flow","E":"Increased hepatic bile flow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At low doses (50 mcg SC) octreotide stimulates small-bowel motility, used in bacterial overgrowth or scleroderma pseudo-obstruction; higher doses inhibit motility. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs are used for acute variceal bleeds and VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: subcutaneous dosing gives a 6 to 12 hour duration of action (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man's ulcerative colitis flare resolves on prednisone. Why should it not be continued long term?","choices":{"A":"Ineffective for active colitis","B":"Ineffective for maintenance","C":"Rapid loss of oral absorption","D":"Frequent drug-induced lupus","E":"Blocks folate absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Corticosteroids are not useful for maintaining remission; aminosalicylates or immunosuppressants are used instead, and steroids are tapered after a response. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inhibit NF-kB and cause Cushing syndrome and osteoporosis with long-term use (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: prednisone induces remission in moderate to severe IBD; oral prednisone is started at 40 to 60 mg/d, and higher doses add toxicity (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman given episodic infliximab for ulcerative colitis loses response and has infusion reactions. What is the likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Rapid hepatic drug clearance","B":"TNF receptor gene mutation","C":"Proteolysis in the gut lumen","D":"Antibodies against the drug","E":"Tolerance at receptor level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antibodies to the antibody can blunt response and raise infusion reaction risk; they are more likely with episodic than scheduled dosing and less likely with concurrent immunomodulators. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: infliximab is a chimeric anti-TNF-alpha monoclonal antibody (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: infusion reactions are more common with second or later infusions (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"The 5-FU metabolite FdUMP exerts its main cytotoxic effect by binding which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase","C":"DNA polymerase alpha","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"Thymidine kinase enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking de novo thymidylate synthesis (\"thymineless death\"); FUTP and FdUTP are also incorporated into RNA and DNA, and the drug acts in S phase. Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate; FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase. Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not FdUMP. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU is a pyrimidine analog bioactivated to 5-FdUMP, lowering dTMP and DNA synthesis, and its effects are enhanced by adding leucovorin (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: 5-FU is given IV because it is broken down rapidly in the GI tract, and its plasma half-life is about 10 to 15 minutes (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate; FdUMP ","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not F"}},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man receiving FOLFOX for stage III colon cancer notices tingling in his fingers and throat when he holds a chilled drink during the infusion. Which toxicity is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute sensory neurotoxicity","B":"Cumulative motor axonopathy","C":"Cryoglobulin vasculitis flare","D":"Hypocalcemic tetany episode","E":"Cerebellar ataxia toxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The platinum agent in FOLFOX causes dose-limiting peripheral sensory neuropathy in two forms: an acute form triggered and worsened by cold, and a chronic cumulative dose-dependent form that is more readily reversible than cisplatin neuropathy. The chronic oxaliplatin neuropathy is cumulative and sensory, whereas cold-triggered symptoms during infusion are the acute form. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, are cell cycle nonspecific, and cause peripheral neuropathy, nephrotoxicity, and ototoxicity (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: FOLFOX is the most widely used first-line regimen for metastatic colorectal cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The chronic oxaliplatin neuropathy is cumulative and sensory"}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"Before cetuximab is added for metastatic colorectal cancer, the tumor must show which molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"HER2 gene amplification","B":"Wild-type KRAS and NRAS","C":"High EGFR protein expression","D":"BRAF V600E mutation","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR; its efficacy is restricted to tumors with wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), because a mutant RAS signals downstream of the blocked receptor. HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cetuximab. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies cause rash, elevated LFTs, and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: because cetuximab is IgG1, part of its antitumor effect may be immune mediated (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cet"}},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which orally available prodrug is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase within tumor tissue?","choices":{"A":"Gemcitabine","B":"Cytarabine","C":"Methotrexate","D":"Hydroxyurea","E":"Capecitabine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Capecitabine has 70% to 80% oral bioavailability; liver carboxylesterase and cytidine deaminase form 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, which thymidine phosphorylase (high in colorectal tumors) converts to 5-FU. It is paired with oxaliplatin as CAPOX. Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is not converted to 5-FU. Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine is the prodrug of 5-FU listed with the pyrimidine antimetabolites (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: CAPOX is used first line in metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for stage III colon cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate re","D":"Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a f"}},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which DNA cross-linking agent has myelosuppression, with much less renal and GI toxicity than cisplatin, as its main dose-limiting effect?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Carboplatin","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Busulfan"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carboplatin shares the mechanism, resistance, and spectrum of cisplatin, but its dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression with significantly less renal and GI toxicity, so it is easier to give and has replaced cisplatin in many regimens. Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and emesis, with minimal myelosuppression. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, and cisplatin nephrotoxicity can be prevented with amifostine (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: platinum agents bind mainly the N7 position of guanine and work in all cell cycle phases (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and "}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anti-EGFR antibody is a fully human IgG2 with fewer infusion reactions?","choices":{"A":"Panitumumab","B":"Cetuximab","C":"Necitumumab","D":"Trastuzumab","E":"Pertuzumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Panitumumab is a fully human IgG2 anti-EGFR antibody, so it is not expected to have immune-mediated effects, and infusion reactions are much less common than with chimeric cetuximab; acneiform rash is a main adverse effect. Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe infusion reactions. Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an IgG2. Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR. Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, not EGFR. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -umab ending marks a human monoclonal antibody and -ximab a chimeric human-mouse one (First Aid Pharmacology, biologic agents). COMLEX: necitumumab is an IgG1 anti-EGFR antibody used for squamous NSCLC and carries thromboembolic risk (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe in","C":"Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an","D":"Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR.","E":"Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, no"}},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage III colon cancer gets her first cycle of an IV fluoropyrimidine with leucovorin and a platinum agent. Within a week she has severe mouth ulcers, profuse diarrhea, and confusion; absolute neutrophil count is 300/µL (reference 1,500–8,000/µL). Deficiency of which enzyme best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Thiopurine methyltransferase","B":"Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase","C":"Uridine glucuronosyltransferase","D":"Hypoxanthine phosphoribosyl transferase","E":"Cytidine deaminase enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most of an administered 5-FU dose is catabolized by DPD. Partial or complete DPD deficiency, an autosomal recessive trait seen in up to 5% of cancer patients (Katzung Ch. 54 elsewhere cites up to 10% of the North American population), causes severe toxicity with the triad of myelosuppression, GI toxicity (diarrhea, mucositis), and neurotoxicity. TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicity. UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of the fluoropyrimidine with oxaliplatin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU causes myelosuppression and is used for colon and pancreatic cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: not every DPD-deficient patient presents with this classic triad (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicit","C":"UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man receives FOLFIRI for metastatic colon cancer. Two hours into the infusion he has abdominal cramps, watery stools, sweating, and salivation. Which drug best treats this early reaction?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Atropine","D":"Ondansetron","E":"Metoclopramide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Irinotecan causes two forms of diarrhea: an early form within 24 hours that is a cholinergic event effectively treated with atropine, and a late form 2 to 10 days later that may cause severe electrolyte imbalance and dehydration. Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this early cholinergic reaction responds to atropine. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: irinotecan and topotecan block topoisomerase I and cause severe myelosuppression and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: irinotecan and its metabolite are cleared mainly in bile and feces, so liver dysfunction requires a dose reduction (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this"}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with metastatic nonseminomatous testicular cancer receives cisplatin, bleomycin, and a semisynthetic mayapple-root derivative. The derivative acts mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Topoisomerase II blockade","B":"Topoisomerase I blockade","C":"Microtubule stabilization","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking","E":"DNA polymerase blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin from the mayapple root, complexes with topoisomerase II and DNA, preventing religation of double-strand breaks and impairing DNA synthesis. It is active in germ cell and lung cancers; renal dysfunction needs dose reduction. Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and topotecan, not etoposide. Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the mayapple-derived etoposide. Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, already in the regimen, not etoposide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: topoisomerase II inhibitors cause myelosuppression and alopecia and treat testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: topoisomerase inhibitors arrest cells in the S and G2 phases (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and ","C":"Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the ","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, alre"}},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with metastatic colorectal cancer is starting second-line therapy. Her oncologist adds to FOLFIRI a humanized antibody that binds circulating VEGF-A. Which drug was added?","choices":{"A":"Cetuximab","B":"Necitumumab","C":"Trastuzumab","D":"Bevacizumab","E":"Rituximab"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bevacizumab binds all forms of VEGF-A and keeps it from reaching VEGF receptors; it combines with 5-FU-, irinotecan-, and oxaliplatin-based therapy in metastatic colorectal cancer; unlike the anti-EGFR antibodies, it targets the ligand rather than EGFR. Hypertension, arterial thromboembolism, GI perforation, and proteinuria are key risks. Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humanized anti-VEGF-A antibody. Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC, not VEGF-A. Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the anti-VEGF antibody also treats renal cell carcinoma, NSCLC, and angioproliferative retinopathy, with hemorrhage and blood clots as adverse effects (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: it does not appear to worsen the usual toxicities of cytotoxic chemotherapy (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humaniz","B":"Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC,","C":"Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A."}},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has metastatic colon cancer whose cells lack mismatch repair function and resist the older platinum drugs in culture. Which DNA cross-linking agent is least affected by this resistance?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Busulfan","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Oxaliplatin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tumors resistant to cisplatin or carboplatin through mismatch repair defects are not cross-resistant to oxaliplatin, which is used with 5-FU and leucovorin (FOLFOX) in metastatic and adjuvant colorectal cancer. Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch repair-deficient tumors resist. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds treat GI, testicular, bladder, ovarian, and lung tumors (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: oxaliplatin regimens are also active in pancreatic, gastroesophageal, and hepatocellular cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch r"}},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man on an anti-EGFR antibody plus FOLFOX for metastatic colorectal cancer develops an acneiform facial rash, muscle cramps, and tremor. Which lab abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum magnesium","B":"High serum potassium","C":"Low serum sodium","D":"High serum calcium","E":"Low serum phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypomagnesemia and acneiform skin rash are the two main adverse effects shared by cetuximab and panitumumab; neuromuscular irritability such as cramps and tremor fits low magnesium. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: cetuximab given with radiation for head and neck cancer carries a small (1%) sudden-death risk and a black-box warning (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman on an oral fluoropyrimidine for colon cancer has red, painful, peeling palms and soles. What is this reaction?","choices":{"A":"Acneiform drug eruption","B":"Fixed drug eruption","C":"Contact dermatitis","D":"Hand-foot syndrome","E":"Erythema multiforme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diarrhea and hand-foot syndrome are the main toxicities of capecitabine; the 5-FU it forms causes the same palmar-plantar reaction. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU adverse effects include myelosuppression and palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: myelosuppression, mucositis, and alopecia are less common with the oral prodrug than with IV 5-FU (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with metastatic colorectal cancer receiving an anti-VEGF-A antibody needs elective hernia repair. Why is surgery delayed after stopping the drug?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged neuromuscular block","B":"Impaired wound healing","C":"Raised anesthetic drug levels","D":"Adrenal insufficiency risk","E":"Reduced platelet production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The main safety concerns with anti-VEGF-A therapy include hypertension, arterial thromboembolic events, wound healing complications, GI perforation, and proteinuria. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-VEGF antibody therapy causes hemorrhage and blood clots (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: postoperative chemotherapy usually begins 4 to 6 weeks after surgery to let the incision heal (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman starts FOLFIRI. Which enzyme converts irinotecan to its 1000-fold more potent active metabolite?","choices":{"A":"Carboxylesterase","B":"Cytidine deaminase","C":"Thymidine phosphorylase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Monoamine oxidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Irinotecan is a prodrug converted mainly in the liver by carboxylesterase to SN-38, a topoisomerase I poison about 1000-fold more potent than the parent drug. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -tecan ending marks a topoisomerase I inhibitor (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: myelosuppression and diarrhea are the two most common adverse events of FOLFIRI's camptothecin (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung"},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with small cell lung cancer is prescribed an epipodophyllotoxin whose oral dose is about twice its IV dose. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Irinotecan","B":"Topotecan","C":"Etoposide","D":"Vincristine","E":"Paclitaxel"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Etoposide has IV and oral forms; oral bioavailability is about 50%, so oral dosing is twice IV dosing, and 30% to 50% is excreted in urine. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the epipodophyllotoxins treat leukemia and lymphoma as well as testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: teniposide is the related epipodophyllotoxin listed with it (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman whose metastatic colorectal cancer progressed on first-line therapy starts an IgG1 antibody that targets VEGFR-2 itself rather than a VEGF ligand. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Ramucirumab","B":"Ziv-aflibercept","C":"Cetuximab","D":"Regorafenib","E":"Pembrolizumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ramucirumab is an IgG1 antibody against VEGFR-2, the main receptor mediating tumor angiogenesis; ziv-aflibercept is a decoy fusion protein that traps VEGF-A, VEGF-B, and PlGF. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: monoclonal antibodies (-mab) target overexpressed cell surface receptors, while -ib small molecules act inside cells (First Aid Pharmacology, drug names). COMLEX: anti-VEGF agents raise blood pressure and the risk of GI perforation (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man whose metastatic colorectal cancer progressed on 5-FU starts an oral combination in which one component blocks breakdown of the other. Which combination is this?","choices":{"A":"Leucovorin-fluorouracil","B":"Irinotecan-cetuximab","C":"Gemcitabine-cisplatin","D":"Cytarabine-daunorubicin","E":"Trifluridine-tipiracil"},"correct":"E","explanation":"TAS-102 combines trifluridine, a weak TS inhibitor incorporated into DNA, with tipiracil, which inhibits thymidine phosphorylase so trifluridine levels stay higher; it works in 5-FU-resistant and RAS-mutant tumors, with neutropenia as the main dose-limiting toxicity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrimidine analogs such as 5-FU and cytarabine interfere with DNA synthesis (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: TAS-102 also causes diarrhea, nausea, fatigue, and anorexia (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true}]};
